{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-8405c17bcca80494", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\t                                                                                                                                                 August 4, 2026\n2\t\n3\t\n4\t\n5\t\n6\t                                        Can Foundation Models Hear What Made That Sound?\n7\t                                        A Tiered Benchmark of Audio-Language Models and Traditional Classifiers for\n8\t                                        Closed-Set Sound Source Identification\n9\t\n10\t                                        Sajjad Abdoli1,*,†1 , Ghassan Al-Sumaidaee1,*,†2 , Ahmad ElShiekh1,*3 ,                          Ahmed Rashad1\n11\t                                          1           *                                                      †\n12\t                                              Perle       Equal contribution; names sorted alphabetically.       Corresponding authors\n13\t                                        sajjad@perle.ai           ghassan.al-sumaidaee@perle.ai\n14\t                                        mad.elshiekh@perle.ai            ahmed@perle.ai\n15\t\n16\t                                        We benchmark eleven audio classification methods — five task-aware closed-set LLMs (four Gemini\n17\t                                        models plus open-weight Kimi-Audio-7B-Instruct), four fixed-vocabulary taggers (YAMNet, PANNs,\n18\t                                        Whisper-AT, SSLAM), a zero-shot audio-text model (CLAP), and an audio-grounded LLM (BAT) —\n19\tarXiv:2608.02397v1 [cs.SD] 3 Aug 2026\n20\t\n21\t\n22\t\n23\t\n24\t                                        on a closed-set sound-source identification task over 2,242 clips spanning 23 fine-grained classes (11\n25\t                                        categories). Since these methods differ fundamentally in how they receive the task and how outputs are\n26\t                                        scored, we group them into four evaluation tiers rather than one leaderboard, reporting macro Precision,\n27\t                                        Recall, F1, and false-negative rate per tier. The best model (Gemini-3.1-Pro-Preview) reaches 85.6%\n28\t                                        category-level and 56.7% fine-grained F1; Kimi-Audio is competitive for its size (67.5%/32.9%) but fails\n29\t                                        to answer 1.6% of samples. SSLAM and CLAP match or exceed the best closed-set model at the category\n30\t                                        level without seeing the candidate list, but fall behind at the fine-grained level. Analyzing the Gemini\n31\t                                        models’ chain-of-thought across 8,968 responses, we find response length does not predict accuracy, an\n32\t                                        apparent ”holistic judgment beats detailed analysis” effect is better explained as a diﬀiculty confound, and\n33\t                                        wrong answers are stated confidently 92–100% of the time. We report full per-class confusion matrices\n34\t                                        and metrics for all eleven methods, identify the structural error modes behind most of the accuracy loss\n35\t                                        between granularities, and give practical guidance for choosing among these method families.\n36\t\n37\t                                        Keywords: environmental sound classification; audio-language models; large language model reasoning;\n38\t                                        closed-set audio classification; benchmark evaluation methodology\n39\t\n40\t\n41\t\n42\t\n43\t                                        1. Introduction\n44\t                                          Identifying the source of an everyday sound — a doorbell, a siren, a running tap — is a task humans\n45\t                                        perform almost effortlessly, but it has historically required purpose-built audio classifiers [1] trained on fixed\n46\t                                        label vocabularies drawn from ontologies such as AudioSet [6]. The recent emergence of general-purpose\n47\t                                        multimodal large language models (LLMs) with native audio input, alongside a new generation of audio-\n48\t                                        grounded LLMs [14, 8] and self-supervised audio encoders [2], raises a practical question for anyone building\n49\t                                        a product on top of audio understanding: given a fixed set of sounds we care about, which class of method\n50\t                                        should we use, and how much can we trust its answer?\n51\t                                           This is harder to answer cleanly than it first appears, because these method families are not interchangeable\n52\t                                        in how they are evaluated. A multimodal LLM can be handed an explicit list of candidate answers and asked\n53\t                                        to pick one; a fixed-vocabulary audio tagger has no notion of a candidate list at all and must be scored\n54\t                                        by mapping its native output vocabulary onto the target taxonomy after the fact; a zero-shot audio-text\n55\t                                           1 Corresponding author: sajjad@perle.ai\n56\t                                           2 Corresponding author: ghassan.al-sumaidaee@perle.ai; ORCID: 0000-0002-5536-0252\n57\t                                           3 ORCID: 0009-0001-6837-6202; current contact: ahmadelshiekh9@gmail.com\n58\t\n59\t\n60\t\n61\t\n62\t                                        © 2026 Perle. All rights reserved.                                                                                   1\n63\t\f                                                                                                 August 4, 2026\n64\t\n65\t\n66\t\n67\tsimilarity model is given natural-language descriptions of the candidates and ranks them by embedding\n68\tsimilarity; and an audio-grounded LLM fine-tuned primarily for open-ended dialogue may produce free text\n69\tthat has to be graded by a second model. Comparing raw accuracy numbers across these four setups risks\n70\tconflating ”this method understood the sound better” with ”this method’s task was easier.”\n71\t   We address this by running all eleven methods on the identical 2,242-sample, 23-class balanced dataset, and\n72\tby treating the four evaluation paradigms as distinct tiers rather than collapsing them into one leaderboard.\n73\tWe report standard per-class detection metrics (macro Precision, Recall/TPR, F1, FNR) rather than raw\n74\taccuracy, since these are the conventional reporting choice in the environmental sound classification (ESC)\n75\tliterature [13] and remain well-defined even for the one method (BAT) for which a full multiclass confusion\n76\tmatrix cannot be constructed. We further exploit the fact that the four LLMs in our study produce free-text\n77\tchain-of-thought reasoning, and run a secondary LLM-based classification pass over all 8,968 saved responses\n78\tto characterize how these models reason their way to an answer — not just whether they are right.\n79\t  The main contributions of this paper are:\n80\t\n81\t• A four-tier evaluation framework for comparing task-aware closed-set selection, task-agnostic fixed-\n82\t  vocabulary tagging, zero-shot audio-text similarity, and open-vocabulary LLM-judged scoring on the\n83\t  same underlying data, with an explicit accounting of why cross-tier comparisons must be read directionally\n84\t  rather than as a strict ranking.\n85\t• A full benchmark of eleven methods across four families on a 2,242-sample, 23-class (11-category) closed-\n86\t  set sound-source identification task, with complete per-class Precision/Recall/F1/TPR/FNR metrics and\n87\t  confusion matrices for every method at both granularities.\n88\t• An analysis of 8,968 LLM chain-of-thought traces showing that (a) response length does not predict\n89\t  fine-grained accuracy, (b) an apparent strategy-accuracy correlation is confounded by which classes elicit\n90\t  which strategy, and (c) none of the four LLMs reliably signals uncertainty when wrong, with 92–100% of\n91\t  incorrect answers phrased with confident language.\n92\t\n93\t\n94\t2. Background and Related Work\n95\t  Environmental sound classification (ESC) is typically framed as a fixed-vocabulary multiclass or multil-\n96\tabel problem: a model is trained (or evaluated) against a closed ontology such as the 527-class AudioSet\n97\tontology [6] or the 50-class ESC-50 dataset [13]. Classical approaches to this problem are convolutional or\n98\ttransformer audio taggers trained end-to-end on labeled audio, of which YAMNet [9, 10] and PANNs [12]\n99\tare widely used pretrained baselines. More recent work adapts speech-recognition backbones for tagging\n100\t(Whisper-AT [7]) or introduces self-supervised pretraining objectives specifically designed for overlapping,\n101\tpolyphonic soundscapes (SSLAM [2]).\n102\t   A parallel line of work moves away from fixed vocabularies entirely. Contrastive audio-text models such as\n103\tCLAP [3] embed audio and natural-language descriptions into a shared space, enabling zero-shot classification\n104\tagainst arbitrary text prompts rather than a fixed label set. Most recently, general-purpose multimodal\n105\tLLMs with native audio understanding (e.g., the Gemini family [5]) and audio-grounded instruction-tuned\n106\tLLMs such as LTU [8], BAT [14], and Kimi-Audio [11] reframe audio classification as a language generation\n107\tor selection task, in principle allowing free-form reasoning before an answer is produced. Kimi-Audio in\n108\tparticular is an open-weight 7B audio-native LLM (an audio tokenizer, a Qwen2.5-based transformer core,\n109\tand an audio detokenizer for optional speech output), distinguishing it architecturally from both the much\n110\tlarger proprietary Gemini models and BAT’s LoRA-adapted encoder-plus-frozen-LLM design.\n111\t  All three of Kimi-Audio, BAT, and (almost certainly) Gemini share a common architectural commit-\n112\tment despite their differences: a separately pretrained audio or speech encoder converts the raw signal into\n113\ta compressed representation before the LLM ever sees it. Concurrent work by Fan et al. [4] (Mel-LLM)\n114\tquestions whether this encoder is necessary at all, removing it entirely and projecting lightly-processed\n115\tMel-spectrogram patches directly into the LLM via a single linear layer — the speech-domain analogue of\n116\tencoder-free vision-language modeling. Their central finding is a trade-off directly relevant to sound-source\n117\tidentification: the encoder-free model shows the largest gains on tasks where the answer is carried by low-level\n118\t\n119\t\n120\tSound Source Identification Benchmark                                                                         2\n121\t\f                                                                                                 August 4, 2026\n122\t\n123\t\n124\t\n125\tacoustic structure rather than lexical content (including a 50-class environmental sound classification bench-\n126\tmark, ESC-50 [13], where it improves substantially over an otherwise-comparable encoder-based baseline),\n127\twhile remaining weaker on knowledge-intensive spoken question answering. This suggests that how much\n128\tan audio-LLM’s front end compresses toward semantic abstraction, versus preserving raw acoustic detail, is\n129\titself a variable worth manipulating for tasks like ours — a possibility we return to in Section 6.4.\n130\t   These four families — fixed-vocabulary taggers, zero-shot audio-text models, general-purpose multimodal\n131\tLLMs, and audio-grounded instruction-tuned LLMs — differ in a way that matters for benchmarking: a\n132\tfixed-vocabulary tagger has no mechanism for accepting an externally-defined candidate list at inference\n133\ttime, while an LLM has no native scoring mechanism over a fixed label set without first being given one\n134\tas part of the prompt. Comparing across families therefore risks conflating a model’s audio understanding\n135\twith how much task information it was given. This is why our study deliberately keeps the underlying\n136\taudio, ground truth, and taxonomy identical across all eleven methods (Section 4), but treats the resulting\n137\tcomparison as tiered by task-awareness and scoring mechanism rather than flat, making the source of any\n138\tgiven accuracy gap between methods (audio understanding versus task framing) explicit rather than implicit.\n139\t\n140\t2.1. Other General-Purpose Multimodal LLMs Considered\n141\t\n142\t  Beyond the four Gemini models and Kimi-Audio, Tier A of this study (Section 4) excludes several other\n143\tgeneral-purpose multimodal LLMs we investigated, because we could not confirm they support native audio\n144\tinput at the time of this study:\n145\t\n146\t• Anthropic’s Claude models do not accept audio as an input modality at all (text and image only, per\n147\t  Anthropic’s own model overview4 — “All current Claude models support text and image input”), so no\n148\t  Claude model could be included in Tier A.\n149\t• OpenAI’s frontier reasoning models (the GPT-5.x family) likewise do not accept audio input: Ope-\n150\t  nAI’s own model documentation lists audio as “Not supported” for GPT-55 . OpenAI’s dedicated audio\n151\t  models (gpt-audio, gpt-audio-mini) do accept audio, per OpenAI’s audio-model guide6 , and were\n152\t  smoke-tested on a small sample of this benchmark, but were not scaled to the full 2,242-sample run\n153\t  reported here and are therefore omitted from Tier A.\n154\t• Meta’s Muse Spark, released via the Meta Model API, is advertised as a multimodal reasoning model,\n155\t  but Meta’s own developer documentation7 lists only text, image, video, and PDF as supported input\n156\t  modalities — audio is not listed. We tested this directly by attempting to upload one of our benchmark\n157\t  audio clips to Meta’s consumer-facing assistant at meta.ai, which rejected the file outright with “Unsup-\n158\t  ported file format. Please upload an image.” With the primary documentation not listing audio support\n159\t  and a direct upload attempt rejecting audio files entirely, we did not include Muse Spark in this study.\n160\t• Moonshot AI’s Kimi K3 (the general-purpose chat model, distinct from Kimi-Audio) likewise does\n161\t  not accept audio: its oﬀicial documentation overview8 and K3 quickstart guide9 list only text, image,\n162\t  and video as supported inputs, with no audio modality documented anywhere in the platform’s indexed\n163\t  documentation. We note this explicitly because both models share a developer and a name prefix; only\n164\t  Kimi-Audio, a separate, dedicated open-weight audio foundation model [11], was included in this study\n165\t  (Tier A).\n166\t\n167\t  We flag this explicitly because it is a real constraint on how far the Tier A comparison generalizes: it spans\n168\tfour Gemini model versions and one open-weight audio-native model, not the full landscape of general-purpose\n169\tmultimodal LLMs, and should be read accordingly.\n170\t  4 https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/models/overview\n171\t  5 https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/gpt-5\n172\t  6 https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/audio\n173\t  7 https://ai.developer.meta.com/docs/getting-started/models\n174\t  8 https://platform.kimi.ai/docs/overview\n175\t  9 https://platform.kimi.ai/docs/guide/kimi-k3-quickstart\n176\t\n177\t\n178\t\n179\t\n180\tSound Source Identification Benchmark                                                                         3\n181\t\f                                                                                                     August 4, 2026\n182\t\n183\t\n184\t\n185\t3. Data\n186\t3.1. Collection and Class Structure\n187\t   The benchmark dataset consists of 2,242 short audio clips drawn from a curated, balanced subset of\n188\teveryday sound recordings. Each clip is labeled with a sound_type (broad category) and a sub_category\n189\t(fine-grained sub-type); the unique combination of the two fields defines 23 fine-grained classes, which collapse\n190\tinto 11 broader categories. Table 1 lists both levels; the full 23-class list with its lettered candidate mapping\n191\tis given in Appendix A.\n192\t\n193\t3.2. Dataset Summary\n194\t  The dataset is balanced at approximately 100 samples per fine-grained class, with one exception (Siren -\n195\tOther, 42 samples) reflecting natural data availability rather than a sampling choice.\n196\t\n197\tTable 1: Dataset structure. All 2,242 samples were attempted by all eleven methods.\n198\t\n199\t Property                                                                             Value\n200\t\n201\t Total samples                                                                        2,242\n202\t Category-level classes (sound_type)                                                   11\n203\t Fine-grained classes (sound_type + sub_category)                                      23\n204\t Samples per fine-grained class                                                ∼100 (42–104)\n205\t Methods evaluated                                                                     11\n206\t Samples evaluated per method                                 2,242 (100% coverage; 0 unresolved errors for ten\n207\t                                                              methods, 36/2,242 unresolved for Kimi-Audio —\n208\t                                                                                Section 4)\n209\t\n210\t\n211\t\n212\t4. Methodology\n213\t4.1. Study Design\n214\t  Every method was evaluated on the identical 2,242-sample set and scored against the identical ground\n215\ttruth at both the category and fine-grained level. What differs across methods is how each one is given the\n216\ttask and how its raw output is converted into a predicted class. We group the eleven methods into four\n217\tevaluation tiers along these two axes.\n218\t\n219\t4.1.1. Tier A — Task-aware closed-set, exact match.Four Gemini models (gemini-2.5-flash,\n220\tgemini-2.5-pro, gemini-3.5-flash, gemini-3.1-pro-preview) were each given the audio clip together\n221\twith a fixed, lettered list of all 23 candidate labels (e.g., A. Appliance Alarm - Appliance Alarm, …, W.\n222\tYell - Yell), an explicit instruction to reason freely (chain-of-thought), and a requirement to commit to\n223\texactly one letter as the final answer (full prompt text in Appendix B.1). Scoring is exact string match\n224\tbetween the predicted and true label at each granularity.\n225\t   We additionally tested Kimi-Audio-7B-Instruct [11], an open-weight audio-native LLM, with the iden-\n226\ttical prompt format. Unlike BAT (Tier D), Kimi-Audio is instruction-tuned broadly enough to follow the\n227\tclosed-set letter-selection task, making it a genuine Tier A member rather than a forced fallback to open-\n228\tvocabulary scoring. In practice its compliance with the ”share your reasoning” instruction was inconsistent:\n229\tthe large majority of responses were a bare letter with no reasoning text at all, a minority included full\n230\tchain-of-thought reasoning ending in the requested JSON answer, and 36/2,242 samples (1.6%) produced\n231\tno scoreable answer whatsoever — 32 repetition-loop generations that never concluded (the model restates\n232\ta description in a loop until hitting the generation-length limit) and 4 explicit refusals (e.g., “none of the\n233\tcandidates match this sound”; one instance, “I cannot analyze the sound as there is no audio provided,”\n234\t\n235\t\n236\tSound Source Identification Benchmark                                                                             4\n237\t\f                                                                                                  August 4, 2026\n238\t\n239\t\n240\t\n241\thallucinates the absence of input that was in fact given — the mirror image of the Gemini grounding failure\n242\tin Section 5.5 that hallucinates the presence of input never given, and then answers anyway rather than\n243\tabstaining). These 36 samples are excluded from Kimi-Audio’s reported metrics at both granularities, iden-\n244\ttically to how an audio-file error would be excluded for any other method; unlike BAT’s exclusions, these are\n245\tnot a scoring design choice but genuine non-answers we could not parse a letter from even after two rounds\n246\tof extraction-logic refinement (JSON, LaTeX-boxed, bare-letter, natural-language, and verbatim-candidate-\n247\techo matching) and a same-settings retry that reproduced the identical failures, confirming they are not\n248\ttransient.\n249\t\n250\t4.1.2. Tier B — Task-agnostic fixed-vocabulary, post-hoc mapped.Four models (YAMNet [9],\n251\tPANNs [12], Whisper-AT [7], SSLAM [2]) were run with no knowledge of our 23-class taxonomy at all.\n252\tEach produces a ranked list of scores over its native ∼521–527-class AudioSet vocabulary. We restrict this\n253\tranking to the subset of AudioSet labels with a known mapping into our taxonomy (tagged exact or approxi-\n254\tmate match quality by hand) and take the top-ranked mapped label as the prediction. Because these models\n255\tnever see the candidate set, their effective task is harder — ranking over the full AudioSet ontology — than\n256\tthe 23-way selection given to Tier A.\n257\t\n258\t4.1.3. Tier C — Zero-shot audio-text similarity.Microsoft CLAP [3] is given the same 23 candidates\n259\tas Tier A, but phrased as natural-language descriptions (e.g., ”a police car siren”) rather than lettered\n260\toptions (full candidate phrasings in Appendix B.2), and produces a prediction via cosine similarity between\n261\tthe audio embedding and each candidate’s text embedding, without any generative reasoning step. CLAP\n262\tis never separately queried against an 11-candidate category-level set: as with Tier A, both granularities\n263\tare scored from this single 23-way ranking — Fine-Grained accuracy compares the full top-ranked candidate\n264\tagainst the true label, and Category-Level accuracy compares only its sound_type component against the\n265\ttrue category, rather than from an independently-elicited coarser prediction.\n266\t\n267\t4.1.4. Tier D — Open-vocabulary generation with LLM-judged scoring.BAT [14] (a Spatial-AST\n268\taudio encoder coupled with a LoRA-adapted LLaMA-2-7B via the SLAM-LLM framework) was run in its\n269\tnative open-vocabulary free-text question-answering mode (exact question text in Appendix B.3), since a\n270\tdirect experiment giving BAT the same lettered candidate list as Tier A failed: BAT’s LoRA fine-tuning\n271\tproduces a strong bias toward binary yes/no answers regardless of instruction, and could not be induced to\n272\tselect a letter. BAT’s free-text answers were instead scored by an independent LLM judge (gemini-2.5-\n273\tflash-lite), prompted to determine separately whether the answer correctly identifies the category-level\n274\tand fine-grained sound source, allowing for synonyms and paraphrases (full judge prompt in Appendix B.4).\n275\t\n276\t4.2. Evaluation Metrics\n277\t   For every method and every sample, we treat each of the C classes (C = 11 at the category level, C = 23\n278\tat the fine-grained level) as a one-vs-rest detection problem and compute, per class c: true positives TPc ,\n279\tfalse positives FPc , false negatives FNc , and true negatives TNc . From these we derive\n280\t\n281\t                                             TPc                                  TPc\n282\t                           Precisionc =              ,      Recallc (TPRc ) =             ,                  (1)\n283\t                                           TPc + FPc                            TPc + FNc\n284\t\n285\t                               2 · Precisionc · Recallc                               FNc\n286\t                      F 1c =                            ,   FNRc = 1 − Recallc =              .              (2)\n287\t                                Precisionc + Recallc                                TPc + FNc\n288\t   We report the unweighted (macro) average of each quantity across all C classes as the headline number\n289\tfor a method at a given granularity. We use macro-averaged F1, TPR, and FNR rather than raw accuracy\n290\tas our primary reported metrics, for two reasons. First, this is the conventional reporting choice in the ESC\n291\tliterature [13, 12], where per-class detection rate and false-negative rate are more informative than a single\n292\tpooled accuracy figure when class diﬀiculty is uneven (as it clearly is here — see Section 5). Second, macro\n293\tRecall/TPR and FNR remain well-defined for Tier D (BAT), where no discrete predicted class exists (the\n294\toutput is free text judged correct/incorrect per sample), so no full confusion matrix — and therefore no\n295\t\n296\t\n297\tSound Source Identification Benchmark                                                                         5\n298\t\f                                                                                                  August 4, 2026\n299\t\n300\t\n301\t\n302\tPrecision or F1 — can be constructed; reporting only what is actually measurable for each tier, rather than\n303\tforcing every method into the same metric, is itself part of the tiered-comparison discipline in Section 5. For\n304\tcompleteness and comparability with the accompanying project write-up, raw accuracy for every method is\n305\talso given in Appendix D.\n306\t\n307\t4.2.1. Match-quality tagging for Tier B.Tier B methods never see our 23-class taxonomy; each one tags\n308\taudio using its own native AudioSet vocabulary (∼521–527 classes), and a hand-built lookup table translates\n309\tthe relevant AudioSet labels into our (sound_type, sub_category) pairs. Each entry in that lookup table\n310\tis tagged exact or approximate, describing the quality of the translation itself — not the model’s confidence,\n311\tand not how close a wrong guess was.\n312\t\n313\t• exact: AudioSet has a label that specifically corresponds to our sub-category. For example, the true label\n314\t  Siren - Police matched against YAMNet’s raw tag \"Police car (siren)\", which the lookup table\n315\t  maps to (Siren, Police, exact). AudioSet has a genuinely specific concept here, so a hit reflects the\n316\t  model actually detecting that particular sound.\n317\t• approximate: AudioSet has no label specific enough to express our sub-category, only a broader, generic\n318\t  one, and we chose to map that generic bucket onto the closest available sub-category when building\n319\t  the lookup table. For example, the true label Appliance Alarm - Appliance Alarm matched against\n320\t  YAMNet’s raw tag \"Alarm\", which the lookup table maps to (Appliance Alarm, Appliance Alarm,\n321\t  approximate). AudioSet’s \"Alarm\" label is a single bucket that also covers car alarms, smoke alarms,\n322\t  and alarm clocks; the model is not telling us “this is specifically an appliance,” only “this sounds like some\n323\t  kind of alarm,” and our table is the one deciding that generic signal counts as a match for this particular\n324\t  sub-category.\n325\t\n326\t   Critically, this tag has no effect on scoring. Whether a mapped prediction is exact or approximate, it\n327\tis scored identically to every other prediction in this study: does the translated label string equal the true\n328\tlabel string? A match is full credit regardless of tag; a non-match is a miss regardless of tag — there\n329\tis no partial credit for approximate matches and no penalty either. match_quality is recorded purely as\n330\ta diagnostic column for post-hoc error analysis (e.g., quantifying how many of a method’s correct answers\n331\treflect a genuinely specific detection versus a generic AudioSet bucket defaulting onto the right sub-category),\n332\tnot as an input to any reported metric. The complete lookup table, with every approximate entry and the\n333\treason it could not be tagged exact, is given in Appendix C.\n334\t  For four sub-category distinctions (clap count, doorbell type, knock surface, water-running distance), Au-\n335\tdioSet’s ontology has no label capable of expressing the distinction at all, only a category-level one (e.g. a\n336\tgeneric \"Doorbell\" label with no digital/mechanical/wireless variants). When a Tier B method’s top-ranked\n337\tmapped prediction resolves to one of these category-only entries, it is scored as an ordinary Fine-Grained\n338\tmiss against whichever true sub-category the sample actually had — the same treatment any other incorrect\n339\tprediction receives, with no special exclusion or partial credit. This keeps Tier B’s Fine-Grained metric on\n340\tthe same footing as Tier A and Tier C rather than granting it credit for distinctions its underlying vocabulary\n341\tcannot express.\n342\t\n343\t4.2.2. Reasoning-strategy analysis (Gemini models only).This secondary analysis covers only the four\n344\tGemini models, not Kimi-Audio: it requires a substantial, consistently-produced chain-of-thought to classify,\n345\tand (as noted above) the large majority of Kimi-Audio’s responses are a bare letter with no reasoning text at\n346\tall, making it a poor candidate for this specific pass. We saved the full reasoning trace for all 2,242×4 = 8,968\n347\tGemini responses and passed each one through a second LLM classification pass (gemini-2.5-flash-lite)\n348\tthat labeled its primary reasoning strategy (detailed acoustic analysis, holistic pattern match, process of\n349\telimination, direct assertion, or non-grounded) and its apparent confidence level (high, medium, low, or\n350\thedging), based purely on the language used — not a self-reported score, since the original prompt never\n351\tasked for one. We combined this with simple lexical statistics (word count, hedge-term and acoustic-term\n352\tfrequency) computed directly from the text.\n353\t\n354\t\n355\t\n356\t\n357\tSound Source Identification Benchmark                                                                          6\n358\t\f                                                                                                          August 4, 2026\n359\t\n360\t\n361\t\n362\t5. Results\n363\t5.1. Category-Level Results (11 classes)\n364\t  Table 2 reports macro Precision, Recall (TPR), F1, and FNR for all eleven methods at the category level,\n365\tgrouped by evaluation tier.\n366\t\n367\tTable 2: Category-level (11-class) macro-averaged results, by evaluation tier. N =2,242 for every method except Kimi-\n368\tAudio (N =2,206; 36 samples produced no scoreable answer — see Section 4, marked ‡). BAT (Tier D) has no discrete\n369\tpredicted class, so Precision and F1 are not computable (†); only Recall/TPR (fraction of each true class the judge scored\n370\tcorrect) and FNR are reported.\n371\t\n372\t Tier                 Method                 Precision         Recall (TPR)              F1                 FNR\n373\t\n374\t                      gemini-3.1-pro-          86.4%               87.4%                85.6%               12.6%\n375\t                      preview\n376\t                      gemini-2.5-pro           79.0%               78.3%                77.0%               21.7%\n377\t                      gemini-3.5-flash         78.1%               78.9%                76.7%               21.1%\n378\t A                    kimi-audio-7b-           71.0%               70.3%                67.5%               29.7%\n379\t                      instruct‡\n380\t                      gemini-2.5-flash         69.5%               66.3%                65.7%               33.7%\n381\t\n382\t                      sslam                    87.9%               87.8%                87.3%               12.2%\n383\t                      panns                    84.6%               83.9%                83.2%               16.1%\n384\t B\n385\t                      whisper-at               78.5%               76.8%                76.2%               23.2%\n386\t                      yamnet                   62.2%               53.4%                52.4%               46.6%\n387\t\n388\t C                    clap                     89.1%               87.3%                87.8%               12.7%\n389\t\n390\t D                    bat                       n/a†               57.7%                n/a†                42.3%\n391\t\n392\t\n393\t5.2. Fine-Grained Results (23 classes)\n394\t\n395\t  Table 3 reports the same metrics at the fine-grained level, where the model must distinguish the exact\n396\tsub-category (e.g., Siren - Police vs. Siren - Ambulance), not just the broad category.\n397\t\n398\t\n399\t\n400\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "385d0263-a010-4e49-aaa9-f6ac26986de1", "uuid": "1b65bb8f-7ee3-4647-b3c0-bb4183c6cae4", "timestamp": "2026-08-04T07:02:57.396Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_2608.02397v1.txt", "content": "                                                                                                                                                 August 4, 2026\n\n\n\n\n                                        Can Foundation Models Hear What Made That Sound?\n                                        A Tiered Benchmark of Audio-Language Models and Traditional Classifiers for\n                                        Closed-Set Sound Source Identification\n\n                                        Sajjad Abdoli1,*,†1 , Ghassan Al-Sumaidaee1,*,†2 , Ahmad ElShiekh1,*3 ,                          Ahmed Rashad1\n                                          1           *                                                      †\n                                              Perle       Equal contribution; names sorted alphabetically.       Corresponding authors\n                                        sajjad@perle.ai           ghassan.al-sumaidaee@perle.ai\n                                        mad.elshiekh@perle.ai            ahmed@perle.ai\n\n                                        We benchmark eleven audio classification methods — five task-aware closed-set LLMs (four Gemini\n                                        models plus open-weight Kimi-Audio-7B-Instruct), four fixed-vocabulary taggers (YAMNet, PANNs,\n                                        Whisper-AT, SSLAM), a zero-shot audio-text model (CLAP), and an audio-grounded LLM (BAT) —\narXiv:2608.02397v1 [cs.SD] 3 Aug 2026\n\n\n\n\n                                        on a closed-set sound-source identification task over 2,242 clips spanning 23 fine-grained classes (11\n                                        categories). Since these methods differ fundamentally in how they receive the task and how outputs are\n                                        scored, we group them into four evaluation tiers rather than one leaderboard, reporting macro Precision,\n                                        Recall, F1, and false-negative rate per tier. The best model (Gemini-3.1-Pro-Preview) reaches 85.6%\n                                        category-level and 56.7% fine-grained F1; Kimi-Audio is competitive for its size (67.5%/32.9%) but fails\n                                        to answer 1.6% of samples. SSLAM and CLAP match or exceed the best closed-set model at the category\n                                        level without seeing the candidate list, but fall behind at the fine-grained level. Analyzing the Gemini\n                                        models’ chain-of-thought across 8,968 responses, we find response length does not predict accuracy, an\n                                        apparent ”holistic judgment beats detailed analysis” effect is better explained as a diﬀiculty confound, and\n                                        wrong answers are stated confidently 92–100% of the time. We report full per-class confusion matrices\n                                        and metrics for all eleven methods, identify the structural error modes behind most of the accuracy loss\n                                        between granularities, and give practical guidance for choosing among these method families.\n\n                                        Keywords: environmental sound classification; audio-language models; large language model reasoning;\n                                        closed-set audio classification; benchmark evaluation methodology\n\n\n\n\n                                        1. Introduction\n                                          Identifying the source of an everyday sound — a doorbell, a siren, a running tap — is a task humans\n                                        perform almost effortlessly, but it has historically required purpose-built audio classifiers [1] trained on fixed\n                                        label vocabularies drawn from ontologies such as AudioSet [6]. The recent emergence of general-purpose\n                                        multimodal large language models (LLMs) with native audio input, alongside a new generation of audio-\n                                        grounded LLMs [14, 8] and self-supervised audio encoders [2], raises a practical question for anyone building\n                                        a product on top of audio understanding: given a fixed set of sounds we care about, which class of method\n                                        should we use, and how much can we trust its answer?\n                                           This is harder to answer cleanly than it first appears, because these method families are not interchangeable\n                                        in how they are evaluated. A multimodal LLM can be handed an explicit list of candidate answers and asked\n                                        to pick one; a fixed-vocabulary audio tagger has no notion of a candidate list at all and must be scored\n                                        by mapping its native output vocabulary onto the target taxonomy after the fact; a zero-shot audio-text\n                                           1 Corresponding author: sajjad@perle.ai\n                                           2 Corresponding author: ghassan.al-sumaidaee@perle.ai; ORCID: 0000-0002-5536-0252\n                                           3 ORCID: 0009-0001-6837-6202; current contact: ahmadelshiekh9@gmail.com\n\n\n\n\n                                        © 2026 Perle. All rights reserved.                                                                                   1\n\f                                                                                                 August 4, 2026\n\n\n\nsimilarity model is given natural-language descriptions of the candidates and ranks them by embedding\nsimilarity; and an audio-grounded LLM fine-tuned primarily for open-ended dialogue may produce free text\nthat has to be graded by a second model. Comparing raw accuracy numbers across these four setups risks\nconflating ”this method understood the sound better” with ”this method’s task was easier.”\n   We address this by running all eleven methods on the identical 2,242-sample, 23-class balanced dataset, and\nby treating the four evaluation paradigms as distinct tiers rather than collapsing them into one leaderboard.\nWe report standard per-class detection metrics (macro Precision, Recall/TPR, F1, FNR) rather than raw\naccuracy, since these are the conventional reporting choice in the environmental sound classification (ESC)\nliterature [13] and remain well-defined even for the one method (BAT) for which a full multiclass confusion\nmatrix cannot be constructed. We further exploit the fact that the four LLMs in our study produce free-text\nchain-of-thought reasoning, and run a secondary LLM-based classification pass over all 8,968 saved responses\nto characterize how these models reason their way to an answer — not just whether they are right.\n  The main contributions of this paper are:\n\n• A four-tier evaluation framework for comparing task-aware closed-set selection, task-agnostic fixed-\n  vocabulary tagging, zero-shot audio-text similarity, and open-vocabulary LLM-judged scoring on the\n  same underlying data, with an explicit accounting of why cross-tier comparisons must be read directionally\n  rather than as a strict ranking.\n• A full benchmark of eleven methods across four families on a 2,242-sample, 23-class (11-category) closed-\n  set sound-source identification task, with complete per-class Precision/Recall/F1/TPR/FNR metrics and\n  confusion matrices for every method at both granularities.\n• An analysis of 8,968 LLM chain-of-thought traces showing that (a) response length does not predict\n  fine-grained accuracy, (b) an apparent strategy-accuracy correlation is confounded by which classes elicit\n  which strategy, and (c) none of the four LLMs reliably signals uncertainty when wrong, with 92–100% of\n  incorrect answers phrased with confident language.\n\n\n2. Background and Related Work\n  Environmental sound classification (ESC) is typically framed as a fixed-vocabulary multiclass or multil-\nabel problem: a model is trained (or evaluated) against a closed ontology such as the 527-class AudioSet\nontology [6] or the 50-class ESC-50 dataset [13]. Classical approaches to this problem are convolutional or\ntransformer audio taggers trained end-to-end on labeled audio, of which YAMNet [9, 10] and PANNs [12]\nare widely used pretrained baselines. More recent work adapts speech-recognition backbones for tagging\n(Whisper-AT [7]) or introduces self-supervised pretraining objectives specifically designed for overlapping,\npolyphonic soundscapes (SSLAM [2]).\n   A parallel line of work moves away from fixed vocabularies entirely. Contrastive audio-text models such as\nCLAP [3] embed audio and natural-language descriptions into a shared space, enabling zero-shot classification\nagainst arbitrary text prompts rather than a fixed label set. Most recently, general-purpose multimodal\nLLMs with native audio understanding (e.g., the Gemini family [5]) and audio-grounded instruction-tuned\nLLMs such as LTU [8], BAT [14], and Kimi-Audio [11] reframe audio classification as a language generation\nor selection task, in principle allowing free-form reasoning before an answer is produced. Kimi-Audio in\nparticular is an open-weight 7B audio-native LLM (an audio tokenizer, a Qwen2.5-based transformer core,\nand an audio detokenizer for optional speech output), distinguishing it architecturally from both the much\nlarger proprietary Gemini models and BAT’s LoRA-adapted encoder-plus-frozen-LLM design.\n  All three of Kimi-Audio, BAT, and (almost certainly) Gemini share a common architectural commit-\nment despite their differences: a separately pretrained audio or speech encoder converts the raw signal into\na compressed representation before the LLM ever sees it. Concurrent work by Fan et al. [4] (Mel-LLM)\nquestions whether this encoder is necessary at all, removing it entirely and projecting lightly-processed\nMel-spectrogram patches directly into the LLM via a single linear layer — the speech-domain analogue of\nencoder-free vision-language modeling. Their central finding is a trade-off directly relevant to sound-source\nidentification: the encoder-free model shows the largest gains on tasks where the answer is carried by low-level\n\n\nSound Source Identification Benchmark                                                                         2\n\f                                                                                                 August 4, 2026\n\n\n\nacoustic structure rather than lexical content (including a 50-class environmental sound classification bench-\nmark, ESC-50 [13], where it improves substantially over an otherwise-comparable encoder-based baseline),\nwhile remaining weaker on knowledge-intensive spoken question answering. This suggests that how much\nan audio-LLM’s front end compresses toward semantic abstraction, versus preserving raw acoustic detail, is\nitself a variable worth manipulating for tasks like ours — a possibility we return to in Section 6.4.\n   These four families — fixed-vocabulary taggers, zero-shot audio-text models, general-purpose multimodal\nLLMs, and audio-grounded instruction-tuned LLMs — differ in a way that matters for benchmarking: a\nfixed-vocabulary tagger has no mechanism for accepting an externally-defined candidate list at inference\ntime, while an LLM has no native scoring mechanism over a fixed label set without first being given one\nas part of the prompt. Comparing across families therefore risks conflating a model’s audio understanding\nwith how much task information it was given. This is why our study deliberately keeps the underlying\naudio, ground truth, and taxonomy identical across all eleven methods (Section 4), but treats the resulting\ncomparison as tiered by task-awareness and scoring mechanism rather than flat, making the source of any\ngiven accuracy gap between methods (audio understanding versus task framing) explicit rather than implicit.\n\n2.1. Other General-Purpose Multimodal LLMs Considered\n\n  Beyond the four Gemini models and Kimi-Audio, Tier A of this study (Section 4) excludes several other\ngeneral-purpose multimodal LLMs we investigated, because we could not confirm they support native audio\ninput at the time of this study:\n\n• Anthropic’s Claude models do not accept audio as an input modality at all (text and image only, per\n  Anthropic’s own model overview4 — “All current Claude models support text and image input”), so no\n  Claude model could be included in Tier A.\n• OpenAI’s frontier reasoning models (the GPT-5.x family) likewise do not accept audio input: Ope-\n  nAI’s own model documentation lists audio as “Not supported” for GPT-55 . OpenAI’s dedicated audio\n  models (gpt-audio, gpt-audio-mini) do accept audio, per OpenAI’s audio-model guide6 , and were\n  smoke-tested on a small sample of this benchmark, but were not scaled to the full 2,242-sample run\n  reported here and are therefore omitted from Tier A.\n• Meta’s Muse Spark, released via the Meta Model API, is advertised as a multimodal reasoning model,\n  but Meta’s own developer documentation7 lists only text, image, video, and PDF as supported input\n  modalities — audio is not listed. We tested this directly by attempting to upload one of our benchmark\n  audio clips to Meta’s consumer-facing assistant at meta.ai, which rejected the file outright with “Unsup-\n  ported file format. Please upload an image.” With the primary documentation not listing audio support\n  and a direct upload attempt rejecting audio files entirely, we did not include Muse Spark in this study.\n• Moonshot AI’s Kimi K3 (the general-purpose chat model, distinct from Kimi-Audio) likewise does\n  not accept audio: its oﬀicial documentation overview8 and K3 quickstart guide9 list only text, image,\n  and video as supported inputs, with no audio modality documented anywhere in the platform’s indexed\n  documentation. We note this explicitly because both models share a developer and a name prefix; only\n  Kimi-Audio, a separate, dedicated open-weight audio foundation model [11], was included in this study\n  (Tier A).\n\n  We flag this explicitly because it is a real constraint on how far the Tier A comparison generalizes: it spans\nfour Gemini model versions and one open-weight audio-native model, not the full landscape of general-purpose\nmultimodal LLMs, and should be read accordingly.\n  4 https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/models/overview\n  5 https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/gpt-5\n  6 https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/audio\n  7 https://ai.developer.meta.com/docs/getting-started/models\n  8 https://platform.kimi.ai/docs/overview\n  9 https://platform.kimi.ai/docs/guide/kimi-k3-quickstart\n\n\n\n\nSound Source Identification Benchmark                                                                         3\n\f                                                                                                     August 4, 2026\n\n\n\n3. Data\n3.1. Collection and Class Structure\n   The benchmark dataset consists of 2,242 short audio clips drawn from a curated, balanced subset of\neveryday sound recordings. Each clip is labeled with a sound_type (broad category) and a sub_category\n(fine-grained sub-type); the unique combination of the two fields defines 23 fine-grained classes, which collapse\ninto 11 broader categories. Table 1 lists both levels; the full 23-class list with its lettered candidate mapping\nis given in Appendix A.\n\n3.2. Dataset Summary\n  The dataset is balanced at approximately 100 samples per fine-grained class, with one exception (Siren -\nOther, 42 samples) reflecting natural data availability rather than a sampling choice.\n\nTable 1: Dataset structure. All 2,242 samples were attempted by all eleven methods.\n\n Property                                                                             Value\n\n Total samples                                                                        2,242\n Category-level classes (sound_type)                                                   11\n Fine-grained classes (sound_type + sub_category)                                      23\n Samples per fine-grained class                                                ∼100 (42–104)\n Methods evaluated                                                                     11\n Samples evaluated per method                                 2,242 (100% coverage; 0 unresolved errors for ten\n                                                              methods, 36/2,242 unresolved for Kimi-Audio —\n                                                                                Section 4)\n\n\n\n4. Methodology\n4.1. Study Design\n  Every method was evaluated on the identical 2,242-sample set and scored against the identical ground\ntruth at both the category and fine-grained level. What differs across methods is how each one is given the\ntask and how its raw output is converted into a predicted class. We group the eleven methods into four\nevaluation tiers along these two axes.\n\n4.1.1. Tier A — Task-aware closed-set, exact match.Four Gemini models (gemini-2.5-flash,\ngemini-2.5-pro, gemini-3.5-flash, gemini-3.1-pro-preview) were each given the audio clip together\nwith a fixed, lettered list of all 23 candidate labels (e.g., A. Appliance Alarm - Appliance Alarm, …, W.\nYell - Yell), an explicit instruction to reason freely (chain-of-thought), and a requirement to commit to\nexactly one letter as the final answer (full prompt text in Appendix B.1). Scoring is exact string match\nbetween the predicted and true label at each granularity.\n   We additionally tested Kimi-Audio-7B-Instruct [11], an open-weight audio-native LLM, with the iden-\ntical prompt format. Unlike BAT (Tier D), Kimi-Audio is instruction-tuned broadly enough to follow the\nclosed-set letter-selection task, making it a genuine Tier A member rather than a forced fallback to open-\nvocabulary scoring. In practice its compliance with the ”share your reasoning” instruction was inconsistent:\nthe large majority of responses were a bare letter with no reasoning text at all, a minority included full\nchain-of-thought reasoning ending in the requested JSON answer, and 36/2,242 samples (1.6%) produced\nno scoreable answer whatsoever — 32 repetition-loop generations that never concluded (the model restates\na description in a loop until hitting the generation-length limit) and 4 explicit refusals (e.g., “none of the\ncandidates match this sound”; one instance, “I cannot analyze the sound as there is no audio provided,”\n\n\nSound Source Identification Benchmark                                                                             4\n\f                                                                                                  August 4, 2026\n\n\n\nhallucinates the absence of input that was in fact given — the mirror image of the Gemini grounding failure\nin Section 5.5 that hallucinates the presence of input never given, and then answers anyway rather than\nabstaining). These 36 samples are excluded from Kimi-Audio’s reported metrics at both granularities, iden-\ntically to how an audio-file error would be excluded for any other method; unlike BAT’s exclusions, these are\nnot a scoring design choice but genuine non-answers we could not parse a letter from even after two rounds\nof extraction-logic refinement (JSON, LaTeX-boxed, bare-letter, natural-language, and verbatim-candidate-\necho matching) and a same-settings retry that reproduced the identical failures, confirming they are not\ntransient.\n\n4.1.2. Tier B — Task-agnostic fixed-vocabulary, post-hoc mapped.Four models (YAMNet [9],\nPANNs [12], Whisper-AT [7], SSLAM [2]) were run with no knowledge of our 23-class taxonomy at all.\nEach produces a ranked list of scores over its native ∼521–527-class AudioSet vocabulary. We restrict this\nranking to the subset of AudioSet labels with a known mapping into our taxonomy (tagged exact or approxi-\nmate match quality by hand) and take the top-ranked mapped label as the prediction. Because these models\nnever see the candidate set, their effective task is harder — ranking over the full AudioSet ontology — than\nthe 23-way selection given to Tier A.\n\n4.1.3. Tier C — Zero-shot audio-text similarity.Microsoft CLAP [3] is given the same 23 candidates\nas Tier A, but phrased as natural-language descriptions (e.g., ”a police car siren”) rather than lettered\noptions (full candidate phrasings in Appendix B.2), and produces a prediction via cosine similarity between\nthe audio embedding and each candidate’s text embedding, without any generative reasoning step. CLAP\nis never separately queried against an 11-candidate category-level set: as with Tier A, both granularities\nare scored from this single 23-way ranking — Fine-Grained accuracy compares the full top-ranked candidate\nagainst the true label, and Category-Level accuracy compares only its sound_type component against the\ntrue category, rather than from an independently-elicited coarser prediction.\n\n4.1.4. Tier D — Open-vocabulary generation with LLM-judged scoring.BAT [14] (a Spatial-AST\naudio encoder coupled with a LoRA-adapted LLaMA-2-7B via the SLAM-LLM framework) was run in its\nnative open-vocabulary free-text question-answering mode (exact question text in Appendix B.3), since a\ndirect experiment giving BAT the same lettered candidate list as Tier A failed: BAT’s LoRA fine-tuning\nproduces a strong bias toward binary yes/no answers regardless of instruction, and could not be induced to\nselect a letter. BAT’s free-text answers were instead scored by an independent LLM judge (gemini-2.5-\nflash-lite), prompted to determine separately whether the answer correctly identifies the category-level\nand fine-grained sound source, allowing for synonyms and paraphrases (full judge prompt in Appendix B.4).\n\n4.2. Evaluation Metrics\n   For every method and every sample, we treat each of the C classes (C = 11 at the category level, C = 23\nat the fine-grained level) as a one-vs-rest detection problem and compute, per class c: true positives TPc ,\nfalse positives FPc , false negatives FNc , and true negatives TNc . From these we derive\n\n                                             TPc                                  TPc\n                           Precisionc =              ,      Recallc (TPRc ) =             ,                  (1)\n                                           TPc + FPc                            TPc + FNc\n\n                               2 · Precisionc · Recallc                               FNc\n                      F 1c =                            ,   FNRc = 1 − Recallc =              .              (2)\n                                Precisionc + Recallc                                TPc + FNc\n   We report the unweighted (macro) average of each quantity across all C classes as the headline number\nfor a method at a given granularity. We use macro-averaged F1, TPR, and FNR rather than raw accuracy\nas our primary reported metrics, for two reasons. First, this is the conventional reporting choice in the ESC\nliterature [13, 12], where per-class detection rate and false-negative rate are more informative than a single\npooled accuracy figure when class diﬀiculty is uneven (as it clearly is here — see Section 5). Second, macro\nRecall/TPR and FNR remain well-defined for Tier D (BAT), where no discrete predicted class exists (the\noutput is free text judged correct/incorrect per sample), so no full confusion matrix — and therefore no\n\n\nSound Source Identification Benchmark                                                                         5\n\f                                                                                                  August 4, 2026\n\n\n\nPrecision or F1 — can be constructed; reporting only what is actually measurable for each tier, rather than\nforcing every method into the same metric, is itself part of the tiered-comparison discipline in Section 5. For\ncompleteness and comparability with the accompanying project write-up, raw accuracy for every method is\nalso given in Appendix D.\n\n4.2.1. Match-quality tagging for Tier B.Tier B methods never see our 23-class taxonomy; each one tags\naudio using its own native AudioSet vocabulary (∼521–527 classes), and a hand-built lookup table translates\nthe relevant AudioSet labels into our (sound_type, sub_category) pairs. Each entry in that lookup table\nis tagged exact or approximate, describing the quality of the translation itself — not the model’s confidence,\nand not how close a wrong guess was.\n\n• exact: AudioSet has a label that specifically corresponds to our sub-category. For example, the true label\n  Siren - Police matched against YAMNet’s raw tag \"Police car (siren)\", which the lookup table\n  maps to (Siren, Police, exact). AudioSet has a genuinely specific concept here, so a hit reflects the\n  model actually detecting that particular sound.\n• approximate: AudioSet has no label specific enough to express our sub-category, only a broader, generic\n  one, and we chose to map that generic bucket onto the closest available sub-category when building\n  the lookup table. For example, the true label Appliance Alarm - Appliance Alarm matched against\n  YAMNet’s raw tag \"Alarm\", which the lookup table maps to (Appliance Alarm, Appliance Alarm,\n  approximate). AudioSet’s \"Alarm\" label is a single bucket that also covers car alarms, smoke alarms,\n  and alarm clocks; the model is not telling us “this is specifically an appliance,” only “this sounds like some\n  kind of alarm,” and our table is the one deciding that generic signal counts as a match for this particular\n  sub-category.\n\n   Critically, this tag has no effect on scoring. Whether a mapped prediction is exact or approximate, it\nis scored identically to every other prediction in this study: does the translated label string equal the true\nlabel string? A match is full credit regardless of tag; a non-match is a miss regardless of tag — there\nis no partial credit for approximate matches and no penalty either. match_quality is recorded purely as\na diagnostic column for post-hoc error analysis (e.g., quantifying how many of a method’s correct answers\nreflect a genuinely specific detection versus a generic AudioSet bucket defaulting onto the right sub-category),\nnot as an input to any reported metric. The complete lookup table, with every approximate entry and the\nreason it could not be tagged exact, is given in Appendix C.\n  For four sub-category distinctions (clap count, doorbell type, knock surface, water-running distance), Au-\ndioSet’s ontology has no label capable of expressing the distinction at all, only a category-level one (e.g. a\ngeneric \"Doorbell\" label with no digital/mechanical/wireless variants). When a Tier B method’s top-ranked\nmapped prediction resolves to one of these category-only entries, it is scored as an ordinary Fine-Grained\nmiss against whichever true sub-category the sample actually had — the same treatment any other incorrect\nprediction receives, with no special exclusion or partial credit. This keeps Tier B’s Fine-Grained metric on\nthe same footing as Tier A and Tier C rather than granting it credit for distinctions its underlying vocabulary\ncannot express.\n\n4.2.2. Reasoning-strategy analysis (Gemini models only).This secondary analysis covers only the four\nGemini models, not Kimi-Audio: it requires a substantial, consistently-produced chain-of-thought to classify,\nand (as noted above) the large majority of Kimi-Audio’s responses are a bare letter with no reasoning text at\nall, making it a poor candidate for this specific pass. We saved the full reasoning trace for all 2,242×4 = 8,968\nGemini responses and passed each one through a second LLM classification pass (gemini-2.5-flash-lite)\nthat labeled its primary reasoning strategy (detailed acoustic analysis, holistic pattern match, process of\nelimination, direct assertion, or non-grounded) and its apparent confidence level (high, medium, low, or\nhedging), based purely on the language used — not a self-reported score, since the original prompt never\nasked for one. We combined this with simple lexical statistics (word count, hedge-term and acoustic-term\nfrequency) computed directly from the text.\n\n\n\n\nSound Source Identification Benchmark                                                                          6\n\f                                                                                                          August 4, 2026\n\n\n\n5. Results\n5.1. Category-Level Results (11 classes)\n  Table 2 reports macro Precision, Recall (TPR), F1, and FNR for all eleven methods at the category level,\ngrouped by evaluation tier.\n\nTable 2: Category-level (11-class) macro-averaged results, by evaluation tier. N =2,242 for every method except Kimi-\nAudio (N =2,206; 36 samples produced no scoreable answer — see Section 4, marked ‡). BAT (Tier D) has no discrete\npredicted class, so Precision and F1 are not computable (†); only Recall/TPR (fraction of each true class the judge scored\ncorrect) and FNR are reported.\n\n Tier                 Method                 Precision         Recall (TPR)              F1                 FNR\n\n                      gemini-3.1-pro-          86.4%               87.4%                85.6%               12.6%\n                      preview\n                      gemini-2.5-pro           79.0%               78.3%                77.0%               21.7%\n                      gemini-3.5-flash         78.1%               78.9%                76.7%               21.1%\n A                    kimi-audio-7b-           71.0%               70.3%                67.5%               29.7%\n                      instruct‡\n                      gemini-2.5-flash         69.5%               66.3%                65.7%               33.7%\n\n                      sslam                    87.9%               87.8%                87.3%               12.2%\n                      panns                    84.6%               83.9%                83.2%               16.1%\n B\n                      whisper-at               78.5%               76.8%                76.2%               23.2%\n                      yamnet                   62.2%               53.4%                52.4%               46.6%\n\n C                    clap                     89.1%               87.3%                87.8%               12.7%\n\n D                    bat                       n/a†               57.7%                n/a†                42.3%\n\n\n5.2. Fine-Grained Results (23 classes)\n\n  Table 3 reports the same metrics at the fine-grained level, where the model must distinguish the exact\nsub-category (e.g., Siren - Police vs. Siren - Ambulance), not just the broad category.\n\n\n\n", "numLines": 400, "startLine": 1, "totalLines": 1330}}}
