{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-8133e9deace2e5a6", "type": "tool_result", "content": "400\t\n401\tSound Source Identification Benchmark                                                                                   7\n402\t\f                                                                                                    August 4, 2026\n403\t\n404\t\n405\tTable 3: Fine-grained (23-class) macro-averaged results, by evaluation tier. N =2,242 for every method except Kimi-\n406\tAudio (N =2,206, marked ‡; see Table 2). Row order within Tier A matches Table 2 (category-level F1 rank) for easy\n407\tcross-reference.\n408\t\n409\t Tier                 Method              Precision        Recall (TPR)             F1                FNR\n410\t\n411\t                      gemini-3.1-pro-       60.7%               60.8%              56.7%              39.2%\n412\t                      preview\n413\t                      gemini-2.5-pro        51.2%               53.3%              50.1%              46.7%\n414\t                      gemini-3.5-flash      57.4%               52.2%              47.9%              47.8%\n415\t A                    kimi-audio-7b-        38.7%               39.6%              32.9%              60.4%\n416\t                      instruct‡\n417\t                      gemini-2.5-flash      38.9%               41.2%              37.0%              58.8%\n418\t\n419\t                      sslam                 40.3%               39.6%              38.0%              60.4%\n420\t                      panns                 37.7%               37.0%              35.9%              63.0%\n421\t B\n422\t                      whisper-at            33.6%               31.4%              30.7%              68.6%\n423\t                      yamnet                27.8%               26.1%              23.7%              73.9%\n424\t\n425\t C                    clap                  61.7%               54.2%              50.6%              45.8%\n426\t\n427\t D                    bat                    n/a†               35.4%              n/a†               64.6%\n428\t\n429\t\n430\t   Two patterns stand out immediately. First, within Tier A, gemini-3.1-pro-preview leads at both gran-\n431\tularities by a comfortable margin (8+ F1 points at the category level over the next-best Gemini model);\n432\tkimi-audio-7b-instruct, the one open-weight model in Tier A, lands fourth of five at both the category\n433\tlevel — ahead of gemini-2.5-flash despite being a much smaller, openly-released model, but last of five\n434\tat the fine-grained level, behind all four Gemini models. Second, the category-to-fine-grained F1 drop is\n435\tuniversal and large: every method loses 25–50 F1 points moving from 11-way to 23-way discrimination, and\n436\tthe ranking of methods shifts substantially between the two tables (e.g. CLAP’s fine-grained F1 of 50.6% is\n437\tonly 6.1 points behind the best Gemini model, despite CLAP never performing any generative reasoning).\n438\t\n439\t5.3. Where the Category-to-Fine-Grained Gap Comes From\n440\t\n441\t  The gap is not diffuse noise; it concentrates in a small number of structurally hard distinctions, visible\n442\tconsistently across methods and confirmed in the per-class confusion matrices in Appendix D:\n443\t\n444\t• Water Running - Distant Source is essentially unsolvable for all five Tier A models (F1 ≈ 0\n445\t  throughout, including Kimi-Audio’s F1 = 0.02), with 57–83% of these samples predicted as Water Run-\n446\t  ning - Close Source across all five (Kimi-Audio: 65%). Models correctly detect running water; they\n447\t  cannot judge recording distance from the audio alone.\n448\t• Doorbell - Wireless is similarly unsolvable for four of five Tier A models (all except gemini-\n449\t  2.5-flash), with errors split between Doorbell - Mechanical and Doorbell - Digital, and 11–35%\n450\t  spilling into Appliance Alarm — a wireless doorbell chime is acoustically closer to an electronic alarm\n451\t  tone than to a mechanical door-knocker. Kimi-Audio shows the strongest version of this particular con-\n452\t  fusion (35% of its Doorbell - Wireless predictions land on Appliance Alarm, versus 11–19% for the\n453\t  four Gemini models).\n454\t• Siren sub-types show a model-family default bias. All five Tier A models struggle to separate\n455\t  Ambulance/Fire/Police/ Other, but gemini-3.1-pro-preview and gemini-2.5-pro default toward\n456\t  Police under uncertainty, while gemini-3.5-flash and gemini-2.5-flash default toward Ambulance\n457\t  — the same underlying acoustic confusion, resolved by a different learned prior per model family. Kimi-\n458\t  Audio joins the Ambulance-defaulting group but far more extremely: 98% of its Siren - Police samples\n459\t  and 86% of its Siren - Fire samples are predicted as Siren - Ambulance, effectively collapsing all siren\n460\t  sub-types into a single answer rather than showing a softer bias.\n461\t\n462\t\n463\tSound Source Identification Benchmark                                                                            8\n464\t\f                                                                                                             August 4, 2026\n465\t\n466\t\n467\t\n468\t\n469\tFigure 1: Category-level confusion matrix (row-normalized recall) for gemini-3.1-pro-preview, the best-performing\n470\tTier A model. The full 23-class fine-grained matrix, where the confusions above are visible in detail, is given in Appendix D\n471\t(Figure 6).\n472\t\n473\t\n474\t5.4. Top-K Accuracy (Tier B)\n475\t  Tables 2 and 3 score every method on its single best answer (Top-1). Tier B methods are unique among the\n476\tfour tiers in that they naturally produce a full ranked list of candidates rather than a single forced choice (Tier\n477\tA and Tier C commit to one answer; Tier D produces free text with no ranking at all), so we additionally\n478\task: does the true label appear anywhere in the model’s top-K ranked, taxonomy-mapped predictions, for\n479\tK ∈ {1, 3, 5}? K=1 reproduces the raw accuracy already reported; K=5 is the deepest ranking collected at\n480\tdata-preparation time, so it is the most lenient, best-case figure available from this data.\n481\t\n482\t\n483\t\n484\t\n485\tSound Source Identification Benchmark                                                                                      9\n486\t\f                                                                                                      August 4, 2026\n487\t\n488\t\n489\tTable 4: Top-1/3/5 accuracy for all four Tier B methods, both granularities. N =2,242 throughout.\n490\t\n491\t Method                                   K=1                        K=3                            K=5\n492\t\n493\t Category-Level (11 classes)\n494\t sslam                                   88.4%                       95.9%                          96.9%\n495\t panns                                   84.3%                       94.0%                          96.5%\n496\t whisper-at                              77.8%                       90.3%                          93.7%\n497\t yamnet                                  52.1%                       64.4%                          66.4%\n498\t\n499\t Fine-Grained (23 classes)\n500\t sslam                                   40.1%                       50.3%                          54.2%\n501\t panns                                   37.3%                       48.8%                          53.5%\n502\t whisper-at                              31.7%                       44.5%                          50.8%\n503\t yamnet                                  26.5%                       35.1%                          40.7%\n504\t\n505\t\n506\t  The gap between K=1 and K=5 is large and consistent: every Tier B method gains roughly 14–19 Fine-\n507\tGrained points by K=5 (e.g. SSLAM: 40.1% → 54.2%; Whisper-AT: 31.7% → 50.8%), indicating the correct\n508\tanswer is frequently present in these models’ ranked output even when their single top choice misses. Most\n509\tof that gain arrives by K=3; going from K=3 to K=5 adds a further 4–6 points, a diminishing but still\n510\treal return. At K=5, SSLAM’s Fine-Grained accuracy (54.2%) approaches CLAP’s Top-1 figure (54.5%,\n511\tTable 12), is on par with the strongest non-preview Tier A models (gemini-2.5-pro, 54.1%) and trails\n512\tonly gemini-3.1-pro-preview (61.5%) - though this is not an apples-to-apples comparision: K=5 credits a\n513\tmethod for having the right answer anywhere in five guesses, a substantially easier bar than the single forced\n514\tanswer every other tier is held to.\n515\t  For the detailed per-class view, we focus the K=5 (best-case) confusion matrices in Appendix D, alongside\n516\tthe standard Top-1 matrices, since K=5 is the most informative depth for characterizing what each Tier B\n517\tmethod’s ranked output is actually capable of resolving.\n518\t\n519\t5.5. Reasoning-Strategy Analysis (Gemini Models)\n520\t\n521\t5.5.1. Verbosity does not predict accuracy.Table 5 and Figure 2 show average chain-of-thought response\n522\tlength by model. The most accurate model, gemini-3.1-pro-preview, is also the most concise — nearly\n523\t7× shorter than gemini-2.5-pro, which writes exhaustive, structured, multi-step analyses (87.6% of its\n524\tresponses use explicit numbered steps, vs. 0% for gemini-3.1-pro-preview) without converting that extra\n525\tlength into better fine-grained F1 than the far terser gemini-3.5-flash (Table 3). Response length and\n526\taccuracy are, if anything, inversely related across these four models.\n527\t\n528\tTable 5: Average chain-of-thought response length by model.\n529\t\n530\t                          Model                               Avg. words / response\n531\t                          gemini-3.1-pro-preview                          52\n532\t                          gemini-3.5-flash                                67\n533\t                          gemini-2.5-flash                               269\n534\t                          gemini-2.5-pro                                 360\n535\t\n536\t\n537\t\n538\t\n539\tSound Source Identification Benchmark                                                                            10\n540\t\f                                                                                              August 4, 2026\n541\t\n542\t\n543\t\n544\t\n545\tFigure 2: Average chain-of-thought response length by model (same data as Table 5).\n546\t\n547\t\n548\t5.5.2. An apparent strategy-accuracy pattern is a diﬀiculty confound.Each response was tagged\n549\twith a primary reasoning strategy. Figure 3 shows the distribution: detailed acoustic analysis (explicit\n550\tdiscussion of frequency, timbre, envelope, pitch) dominates for every model, from 78.1% (gemini-2.5-flash)\n551\tup to 95.2% (gemini-3.5-flash) of responses. Holistic pattern match (a quick ”this sounds like X” judgment\n552\twith no feature breakdown) is the clear second-most-common strategy for all four models, but its share varies\n553\tconsiderably: gemini-3.1-pro-preview uses it in 16.5% of responses, three to eight times more often than\n554\tthe other three models (1.9–5.5%). The remaining strategies (process of elimination, direct assertion, non-\n555\tgrounded) are minor and inconsistent across models, appearing in at most 13.9% of any one model’s responses.\n556\t  Naively cross-referencing strategy against fine-grained accuracy (Table 6, visualized in Figure 4) shows\n557\tthe same pattern in all four models: responses tagged holistic pattern match are correct 67.7–79.1% of the\n558\ttime, versus only 39.7–59.1% for detailed acoustic analysis — a 14.3–28.0 point gap in every model, with no\n559\texceptions.\n560\t\n561\t\n562\t\n563\t\n564\tSound Source Identification Benchmark                                                                     11\n565\t\f                                                                                                         August 4, 2026\n566\t\n567\t\n568\t\n569\t\n570\tFigure 3: Primary reasoning strategy distribution by model. Detailed acoustic analysis dominates throughout (78.1–\n571\t95.2%); holistic pattern match is a distant second everywhere, but far more common for gemini-3.1-pro-preview\n572\t(16.5%) than the other three models (1.9–5.5%).\n573\t\n574\t\n575\tTable 6: Fine-grained accuracy by primary reasoning strategy, for the two dominant strategies, per model. n is the\n576\tnumber of responses tagged with that strategy (out of 2,242 per model); the holistic-pattern-match bar is taller than the\n577\tdetailed-acoustic-analysis bar in every model.\n578\t\n579\t Model                            Detailed-analysis acc.      Holistic-match acc. (n)             Gap (points)\n580\t                                           (n)\n581\t\n582\t gemini-3.1-pro-preview                 59.1% (n=1,871)             73.4% (n=369)                      14.3\n583\t gemini-2.5-pro                         53.5% (n=2,122)             79.1% (n=43)                       25.6\n584\t gemini-3.5-flash                       52.4% (n=2,135)             78.8% (n=85)                       26.4\n585\t gemini-2.5-flash                       39.7% (n=1,750)             67.7% (n=124)                      28.0\n586\t\n587\t\n588\t\n589\t\n590\tSound Source Identification Benchmark                                                                                 12\n591\t\f                                                                                                    August 4, 2026\n592\t\n593\t\n594\t\n595\t\n596\tFigure 4: Fine-grained accuracy conditioned on primary reasoning strategy, per model (exact values in Table 6). In\n597\tevery model the holistic-pattern-match bar is taller than the detailed-acoustic-analysis bar.\n598\t\n599\t\n600\t   Read at face value, this looks like ”less analysis produces better answers.” We do not believe that is the\n601\tcorrect interpretation. Restricting the comparison to gemini-3.1-pro-preview — the model with by far the\n602\thighest holistic-match rate (Figure 3) — and conditioning on individual classes (Table 7) shows that the two\n603\tstrategies score about the same within a given class (e.g., 100.0% vs. 98.9% on Yell); the apparent strategy\n604\teffect disappears once class diﬀiculty is held fixed.\n605\t\n606\tTable 7: Fine-grained accuracy by reasoning strategy, within class, for gemini-3.1-pro-preview. These three classes\n607\taccount for 47% of all holistic pattern match responses from this model.\n608\t\n609\t Class                                  Overall accuracy    Detailed-analysis acc.     Holistic-match acc. (n)\n610\t                                                                     (n)\n611\t Yell - Yell                                 99.0%               100.0% (n=9)                 98.9% (n=89)\n612\t Appliance Alarm                             93.0%               90.7% (n=54)                 95.7% (n=46)\n613\t Water Running - Close                       80.0%               78.7% (n=61)                 82.1% (n=39)\n614\t Source\n615\t\n616\t\n617\t  The more plausible explanation, consistent with Table 7: models default to a quick holistic judgment\n618\tprecisely when a sound is unambiguous, and reach for detailed acoustic analysis when it is genuinely hard\n619\tto place. The reasoning strategy is a symptom of per-sample diﬀiculty, not a cause of the outcome — but it\n620\tremains a useful signal: a model defaulting to detailed analysis is implicitly flagging ”this one was hard.”\n621\t\n622\t5.5.3. Confidence calibration is essentially absent.We measured how often a model’s language reads\n623\tas confident even when its final answer is wrong (Table 8, Figure 5). Confidence level is inferred purely from\n624\tphrasing (e.g., ”clearly,” ”definitely” vs. ”possibly,” ”might be”) by the second-pass classifier — models were\n625\tnever asked to self-report a confidence score.\n626\t\n627\t\n628\t\n629\t\n630\tSound Source Identification Benchmark                                                                           13\n631\t\f                                                                                                     August 4, 2026\n632\t\n633\t\n634\tTable 8: Share of wrong fine-grained answers still stated with high-confidence language.\n635\t\n636\t                    Model                                       Wrong answers stated with\n637\t                                                                 high-confidence language\n638\t                    gemini-3.5-flash                                        100.0%\n639\t                    gemini-3.1-pro-preview                                   99.9%\n640\t                    gemini-2.5-pro                                           97.8%\n641\t                    gemini-2.5-flash                                         92.1%\n642\t\n643\t\n644\t\n645\t\n646\tFigure 5: Share of wrong answers still stated with high-confidence language, by model (same data as Table 8).\n647\t\n648\t\n649\t   As Table 8 and Figure 5 show, all four models are essentially uniform on this measure — the range spans\n650\tonly 92.1% to 100.0%, with no model showing meaningfully better calibration than the others. Hedging\n651\tlanguage (”possibly,” ”might,” ”uncertain”) appears less than once per response on average for every model.\n652\tThere is effectively no verbal signal in these chain-of-thought traces that reliably distinguishes a correct\n653\tanswer from an incorrect one; a system that needs to know when to defer to a human cannot obtain that\n654\tsignal from a model’s stated confidence on this task.\n655\t\n656\t\n657\t6. Discussion\n658\t6.1. What Can and Cannot Be Compared Directly\n659\t  The four-tier structure in Section 4 exists because a single flat ranking across all eleven methods would\n660\toverstate precision. The defensible comparisons are within a tier: which Tier A method is best (four Gemini\n661\tmodels plus Kimi-Audio, all given the identical closed-set prompt), and which fixed-vocabulary tagger is best\n662\t(Tier B). Tier C (CLAP) and Tier D (BAT) are each a category of one in this study and are reported for\n663\tdirectional context rather than as ranked competitors.\n664\t   That said, one cross-tier observation is robust enough to be worth surfacing directionally: at the category\n665\tlevel, task-agnostic methods that never saw the candidate list (SSLAM, PANNs in Tier B; CLAP in Tier C)\n666\tmatch or exceed the best task-aware Tier A model’s F1, and PANNs approches it. At the fine-grained level,\n667\tthis reverses — the best Tier A model and CLAP (Tier C) pull ahead of every Tier B tagger by 12+ F1\n668\t\n669\t\n670\tSound Source Identification Benchmark                                                                           14\n671\t\f                                                                                                August 4, 2026\n672\t\n673\t\n674\t\n675\tpoints. A plausible reading is that coarse categorization is largely an acoustic pattern-matching problem, at\n676\twhich purpose-built audio encoders (with or without a candidate list) are already strong, while fine-grained\n677\tsub-type disambiguation increasingly draws on the kind of contextual, elimination-based reasoning that Tier\n678\tA’s language-generation step provides and Tier B’s fixed classification head does not. We stress this is a\n679\thypothesis suggested by the data, not a controlled causal claim, since Tier A and Tier B differ in task\n680\tframing as well as in architecture.\n681\t  Even within Tier A, the five methods are not architecturally uniform: the four Gemini models are large\n682\tproprietary systems of undisclosed scale, while Kimi-Audio-7B-Instruct is a 7B-parameter open-weight model.\n683\tThat it lands mid-pack in Tier A rather than at the bottom is itself informative — task-aware closed-set\n684\tprompting appears to narrow, though not eliminate, the gap between an open 7B audio-native model and\n685\tmuch larger proprietary multimodal systems on this task — but its lower reasoning-instruction compliance\n686\t(Section 4) means its Tier A ranking reflects both classification ability and instruction- following, in a way\n687\tthe four Gemini models’ more consistent compliance does not.\n688\t\n689\t6.2. Practical Guidance\n690\t   For a use case that only needs the broad category (is this a siren or a doorbell?), several method families\n691\tare viable, and a task-agnostic tagger (SSLAM, PANNs) or a zero-shot model (CLAP) may be suﬀicient\n692\twithout needing an LLM call per sample. For a use case needing the exact sub-type, the strongest current\n693\tmethods top out around 51–57% F1, and Tier A (task-aware LLM with an explicit candidate list) or Tier C\n694\t(CLAP) are the strongest available options; expect specific, predictable failure modes — recording-distance\n695\tand directionality judgments, and acoustically adjacent sub-types within a category — rather than random\n696\terror. If an open-weight, self-hostable option is a requirement, Kimi-Audio-7B-Instruct is a usable Tier A\n697\talternative to the Gemini models, at a cost in fine-grained F1 (32.9% vs. 56.7% for the best Gemini model)\n698\tand in reliability (1.6% of samples yield no scoreable answer at all, versus 0% for the Gemini models), both\n699\tworth budgeting for in a production deployment.\n700\t\n701\t6.3. Limitations\n702\t• Tier C’s task framing partially overlaps Tier A’s. CLAP is given the same 23 candidates as the\n703\t  Gemini models (as text), which is part of why its numbers are more directly comparable to Tier A than\n704\t  Tier B’s are; this is noted explicitly rather than treated as a hidden asymmetry, but it means CLAP’s\n705\t  strong fine-grained score should not be read as evidence that zero-shot similarity generally rivals LLM\n706\t  reasoning on unconstrained audio tagging.\n707\t• Tier D’s scoring depends on a judge model. BAT’s free-text answers are graded by gemini-2.5-\n708\t  flash-lite rather than exact match; judge leniency or strictness is a source of variance this study does\n709\t  not independently calibrate against a second judge or human raters.\n710\t• Tier B’s mapping is post-hoc. The AudioSet-to-taxonomy mapping used for YAMNet, PANNs,\n711\t  Whisper-AT, and SSLAM is hand-constructed and tagged exact/approximate at data-preparation time;\n712\t  a different, equally reasonable mapping could shift these numbers somewhat, though the relative ordering\n713\t  within Tier B was stable under manual review.\n714\t• BAT’s spatial-audio design is not exercised here. BAT’s Spatial-AST encoder expects binaural\n715\t  input with directional cues; our clips are effectively mono, so the channel was duplicated rather than\n716\t  fabricating synthetic spatial cues. This is an honest ”no directional information” representation but\n717\t  means BAT is evaluated outside its primary designed use case.\n718\t• Kimi-Audio’s 36 excluded samples are a genuine reliability difference, not a scoring artifact.\n719\t  We confirmed this by re-attempting extraction with three successive rounds of parser improvements and\n720\t  by re-running the 36 samples under identical settings, which reproduced the same failures rather than\n721\t  resolving them (Kimi-Audio’s text decoding is greedy, so a same-settings retry has limited ability to\n722\t  escape a repetition-loop or refusal outcome). We did not pursue a targeted fix (e.g., raising the text\n723\t  repetition penalty, currently uninformatively set to 1.0) specifically for these samples, since doing so\n724\t  would apply different generation settings to a subset of Kimi-Audio’s data than to the rest, undermining\n725\t  the like-for-like comparison this study is built around.\n726\t\n727\t\n728\tSound Source Identification Benchmark                                                                      15\n729\t\f                                                                                                  August 4, 2026\n730\t\n731\t\n732\t\n733\t• Single dataset, single run. All numbers reflect one balanced 23-class dataset and a single evaluation\n734\t  pass per method (no repeated sampling or multiple-seed variance estimates), and the 11-category taxon-\n735\t  omy reflects the specific sound sources of interest to this study rather than a general-purpose acoustic\n736\t  scene ontology.\n737\t\n738\t6.4. Future Work\n739\t  Natural extensions include: a second, independent LLM judge (or a small human-rated subset) to quantify\n740\tTier D’s judge-variance; testing whether few-shot prompting can induce BAT toward closed-set letter selection\n741\trather than its default binary yes/no bias; and extending the tiered framework to acoustic scenes with genuine\n742\tspatial/directional structure, which would let BAT’s native architecture be evaluated on-task rather than\n743\tdegraded to mono input.\n744\t   A further direction motivated by concurrent work (Section 2) is to manipulate the audio front end itself as\n745\tan independent variable within Tier A. All Tier A methods in this study are encoder-based to some degree\n746\t— Kimi-Audio explicitly tokenizes audio through a pretrained Whisper-derived encoder before its LLM core\n747\tever sees it, and Gemini’s proprietary audio pathway is almost certainly similar in spirit. Fan et al. [4] report\n748\tthat removing this encoder entirely (projecting Mel-spectrogram patches directly into the LLM) improves\n749\tperformance specifically on tasks carried by low-level acoustic structure, including environmental sound\n750\tclassification, at some cost to knowledge-intensive tasks. Our own fine-grained results are dominated by\n751\texactly this kind of acoustic discrimination — distinguishing Siren - Police from Siren - Ambulance, or\n752\ta wireless doorbell chime from a mechanical one, plausibly turns on preserved pitch contour and timbre rather\n753\tthan semantic content. Whether an encoder-free architecture narrows Kimi-Audio’s fine-grained gap to the\n754\tGemini models specifically on these acoustically-driven distinctions, or whether Gemini’s advantage instead\n755\treflects sheer model scale or training data, is a testable question our tiered framework is directly set up to\n756\tanswer, given access to a Mel-LLM-style checkpoint evaluated under the same closed-set prompt.\n757\t\n758\t\n759\t7. Conclusion\n760\t   Comparing eleven audio classification methods spanning four fundamentally different evaluation paradigms\n761\ton the identical 2,242-sample, 23-class dataset shows that no single method dominates once category and fine-\n762\tgrained granularity, and task framing, are both accounted for. Task-aware closed-set LLM selection (gemini-\n763\t3.1-pro-preview) gives the best fine-grained F1 (56.7%) of any method tested, but task-agnostic fixed-\n764\tvocabulary taggers and zero-shot audio-text similarity are competitive or superior at the coarser category\n765\tlevel despite never seeing the candidate list. The open-weight Kimi-Audio-7B-Instruct, evaluated under the\n766\tidentical closed-set prompt, reaches a competitive 67.5%/32.9% category/fine- grained F1 for its size, though\n767\twith a measurably higher rate of unscoreable responses (1.6% of samples) than the four Gemini models (0%),\n768\treflecting less consistent instruction-following rather than a scoring artifact. The category-to-fine-grained\n769\taccuracy gap is concentrated in a small number of structurally hard, consistent failure modes rather than\n770\tdiffuse noise, and these generalize to Kimi-Audio, in some cases (Siren sub-types) more extremely than any\n771\tGemini model. Separately, analyzing the Gemini models’ own chain-of-thought reveals that more reasoning\n772\ttext does not buy more accuracy, that an apparent reasoning-strategy effect on accuracy is confounded by\n773\tper-sample diﬀiculty, and — most consequential for downstream use — that these models give almost no\n774\tusable signal, in their stated confidence, of when they are wrong.\n775\t\n776\t\n777\tEthics Statement\n778\t  This study evaluates model performance on a curated set of short environmental sound recordings (e.g.,\n779\tdoorbells, sirens, appliance sounds) and involves no human subjects, personal data, or sensitive content.\n780\t\n781\t\n782\t\n783\t\n784\tSound Source Identification Benchmark                                                                         16\n785\t\f                                                                                              August 4, 2026\n786\t\n787\t\n788\t\n789\tReferences\n790\t [1] Sajjad Abdoli, Patrick Cardinal, and Alessandro Lameiras Koerich. End-to-end environmental sound\n791\t     classification using a 1d convolutional neural network. Expert Systems with Applications, 136:252–263,\n792\t     2019.\n793\t [2] Tony Alex, Sara Atito, Armin Mustafa, Muhammad Awais, and Philip J. B. Jackson. Sslam: Enhancing\n794\t     self-supervised models with audio mixtures for polyphonic soundscapes. In International Conference on\n795\t     Learning Representations (ICLR), 2025.\n796\t [3] Benjamin Elizalde, Soham Deshmukh, and Huaming Wang. Natural language supervision for general-\n797\t     purpose audio representations. In Proceedings of the IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech\n798\t     and Signal Processing (ICASSP), pages 336–340, 2024.\n799\t [4] Ruchao Fan, Yiming Wang, Yuxuan Hu, Bo Ren, Yufei Xia, Xiaofei Wang, Yao Qian, Shujie Liu, and\n800\t     Jinyu Li. Llm can read spectrogram: Encoder-free speech-language modeling, 2026.\n801\t [5] Google Gemini Team, Gheorghe Comanici, Eric Bieber, Mike Schaekermann, et al. Gemini 2.5: Push-\n802\t     ing the frontier with advanced reasoning, multimodality, long context, and next generation agentic\n803\t     capabilities, 2025.\n804\t\n805\t [6] Jort F. Gemmeke, Daniel P. W. Ellis, Dylan Freedman, Aren Jansen, Wade Lawrence, R. Channing\n806\t     Moore, Manoj Plakal, and Marvin Ritter. Audio set: An ontology and human-labeled dataset for audio\n807\t     events. In Proceedings of the IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing\n808\t     (ICASSP), pages 776–780, 2017.\n809\t [7] Yuan Gong, Sameer Khurana, Leonid Karlinsky, and James Glass. Whisper-at: Noise-robust automatic\n810\t     speech recognizers are also strong general audio event taggers. In Proc. Interspeech 2023, pages 2798–\n811\t     2802, 2023.\n812\t [8] Yuan Gong, Hongyin Luo, Alexander H. Liu, Leonid Karlinsky, and James Glass. Listen, think, and\n813\t     understand. In International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR), 2024.\n814\t [9] Google Research. Yamnet. https://github.com/tensorflow/models/tree/master/research/au\n815\t     dioset/yamnet, 2020. Pretrained audio event classification model released in the TensorFlow Models\n816\t     repository, trained on AudioSet using the architecture of Hershey et al. [10].\n817\t[10] Shawn Hershey, Sourish Chaudhuri, Daniel P. W. Ellis, Jort F. Gemmeke, Aren Jansen, R. Channing\n818\t     Moore, Manoj Plakal, Devin Platt, Rif A. Saurous, Bryan Seybold, Malcolm Slaney, Ron J. Weiss, and\n819\t     Kevin W. Wilson. Cnn architectures for large-scale audio classification. In Proceedings of the IEEE\n820\t     International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP), pages 131–135, 2017.\n821\t\n822\t[11] KimiTeam, Ding Ding, Zeqian Ju, Yichong Leng, Songxiang Liu, Tong Liu, Zeyu Shang, Kai Shen, Wei\n823\t     Song, Xu Tan, Heyi Tang, Zhengtao Wang, Chu Wei, Yifei Xin, Xinran Xu, Jianwei Yu, Yutao Zhang,\n824\t     Xinyu Zhou, Y. Charles, et al. Kimi-audio technical report, 2025.\n825\t[12] Qiuqiang Kong, Yin Cao, Turab Iqbal, Yuxuan Wang, Wenwu Wang, and Mark D. Plumbley. Panns:\n826\t     Large-scale pretrained audio neural networks for audio pattern recognition. IEEE/ACM Transactions\n827\t     on Audio, Speech, and Language Processing, 28:2880–2894, 2020.\n828\t[13] Karol J. Piczak. Esc: Dataset for environmental sound classification. In Proceedings of the 23rd ACM\n829\t     International Conference on Multimedia, pages 1015–1018, 2015.\n830\t[14] Zhisheng Zheng, Puyuan Peng, Ziyang Ma, Xie Chen, Eunsol Choi, and David Harwath. Bat: Learning\n831\t     to reason about spatial sounds with large language models. In Proceedings of the 41st International\n832\t     Conference on Machine Learning (ICML), volume 235 of Proceedings of Machine Learning Research,\n833\t     pages 61454–61469, 2024.\n834\t\n835\t\n836\t\n837\tSound Source Identification Benchmark                                                                    17\n838\t\f                                                                                              August 4, 2026\n839\t\n840\t\n841\t\n842\tA. The 23 Fine-Grained Candidate Classes\n843\t  Table 9 lists the exact 23 lettered candidates given to every Tier A (Gemini) model for every sample, and\n844\tto Tier C (CLAP) as natural-language phrasings of the same 23 classes. These collapse into the 11 broader\n845\tCategory-Level classes: Appliance Alarm, Cat Sounds, Clap, Dog Sounds, Doorbell, Hairdryer, Kettle Boiling,\n846\tKnock, Siren, Water Running, Yell.\n847\t\n848\tTable 9: The 23 fine-grained candidate classes.\n849\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "385d0263-a010-4e49-aaa9-f6ac26986de1", "uuid": "bd3016ea-fd7b-4eed-8636-71d2a74459ec", "timestamp": "2026-08-04T07:03:52.256Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_2608.02397v1.txt", "content": "\nSound Source Identification Benchmark                                                                                   7\n\f                                                                                                    August 4, 2026\n\n\nTable 3: Fine-grained (23-class) macro-averaged results, by evaluation tier. N =2,242 for every method except Kimi-\nAudio (N =2,206, marked ‡; see Table 2). Row order within Tier A matches Table 2 (category-level F1 rank) for easy\ncross-reference.\n\n Tier                 Method              Precision        Recall (TPR)             F1                FNR\n\n                      gemini-3.1-pro-       60.7%               60.8%              56.7%              39.2%\n                      preview\n                      gemini-2.5-pro        51.2%               53.3%              50.1%              46.7%\n                      gemini-3.5-flash      57.4%               52.2%              47.9%              47.8%\n A                    kimi-audio-7b-        38.7%               39.6%              32.9%              60.4%\n                      instruct‡\n                      gemini-2.5-flash      38.9%               41.2%              37.0%              58.8%\n\n                      sslam                 40.3%               39.6%              38.0%              60.4%\n                      panns                 37.7%               37.0%              35.9%              63.0%\n B\n                      whisper-at            33.6%               31.4%              30.7%              68.6%\n                      yamnet                27.8%               26.1%              23.7%              73.9%\n\n C                    clap                  61.7%               54.2%              50.6%              45.8%\n\n D                    bat                    n/a†               35.4%              n/a†               64.6%\n\n\n   Two patterns stand out immediately. First, within Tier A, gemini-3.1-pro-preview leads at both gran-\nularities by a comfortable margin (8+ F1 points at the category level over the next-best Gemini model);\nkimi-audio-7b-instruct, the one open-weight model in Tier A, lands fourth of five at both the category\nlevel — ahead of gemini-2.5-flash despite being a much smaller, openly-released model, but last of five\nat the fine-grained level, behind all four Gemini models. Second, the category-to-fine-grained F1 drop is\nuniversal and large: every method loses 25–50 F1 points moving from 11-way to 23-way discrimination, and\nthe ranking of methods shifts substantially between the two tables (e.g. CLAP’s fine-grained F1 of 50.6% is\nonly 6.1 points behind the best Gemini model, despite CLAP never performing any generative reasoning).\n\n5.3. Where the Category-to-Fine-Grained Gap Comes From\n\n  The gap is not diffuse noise; it concentrates in a small number of structurally hard distinctions, visible\nconsistently across methods and confirmed in the per-class confusion matrices in Appendix D:\n\n• Water Running - Distant Source is essentially unsolvable for all five Tier A models (F1 ≈ 0\n  throughout, including Kimi-Audio’s F1 = 0.02), with 57–83% of these samples predicted as Water Run-\n  ning - Close Source across all five (Kimi-Audio: 65%). Models correctly detect running water; they\n  cannot judge recording distance from the audio alone.\n• Doorbell - Wireless is similarly unsolvable for four of five Tier A models (all except gemini-\n  2.5-flash), with errors split between Doorbell - Mechanical and Doorbell - Digital, and 11–35%\n  spilling into Appliance Alarm — a wireless doorbell chime is acoustically closer to an electronic alarm\n  tone than to a mechanical door-knocker. Kimi-Audio shows the strongest version of this particular con-\n  fusion (35% of its Doorbell - Wireless predictions land on Appliance Alarm, versus 11–19% for the\n  four Gemini models).\n• Siren sub-types show a model-family default bias. All five Tier A models struggle to separate\n  Ambulance/Fire/Police/ Other, but gemini-3.1-pro-preview and gemini-2.5-pro default toward\n  Police under uncertainty, while gemini-3.5-flash and gemini-2.5-flash default toward Ambulance\n  — the same underlying acoustic confusion, resolved by a different learned prior per model family. Kimi-\n  Audio joins the Ambulance-defaulting group but far more extremely: 98% of its Siren - Police samples\n  and 86% of its Siren - Fire samples are predicted as Siren - Ambulance, effectively collapsing all siren\n  sub-types into a single answer rather than showing a softer bias.\n\n\nSound Source Identification Benchmark                                                                            8\n\f                                                                                                             August 4, 2026\n\n\n\n\nFigure 1: Category-level confusion matrix (row-normalized recall) for gemini-3.1-pro-preview, the best-performing\nTier A model. The full 23-class fine-grained matrix, where the confusions above are visible in detail, is given in Appendix D\n(Figure 6).\n\n\n5.4. Top-K Accuracy (Tier B)\n  Tables 2 and 3 score every method on its single best answer (Top-1). Tier B methods are unique among the\nfour tiers in that they naturally produce a full ranked list of candidates rather than a single forced choice (Tier\nA and Tier C commit to one answer; Tier D produces free text with no ranking at all), so we additionally\nask: does the true label appear anywhere in the model’s top-K ranked, taxonomy-mapped predictions, for\nK ∈ {1, 3, 5}? K=1 reproduces the raw accuracy already reported; K=5 is the deepest ranking collected at\ndata-preparation time, so it is the most lenient, best-case figure available from this data.\n\n\n\n\nSound Source Identification Benchmark                                                                                      9\n\f                                                                                                      August 4, 2026\n\n\nTable 4: Top-1/3/5 accuracy for all four Tier B methods, both granularities. N =2,242 throughout.\n\n Method                                   K=1                        K=3                            K=5\n\n Category-Level (11 classes)\n sslam                                   88.4%                       95.9%                          96.9%\n panns                                   84.3%                       94.0%                          96.5%\n whisper-at                              77.8%                       90.3%                          93.7%\n yamnet                                  52.1%                       64.4%                          66.4%\n\n Fine-Grained (23 classes)\n sslam                                   40.1%                       50.3%                          54.2%\n panns                                   37.3%                       48.8%                          53.5%\n whisper-at                              31.7%                       44.5%                          50.8%\n yamnet                                  26.5%                       35.1%                          40.7%\n\n\n  The gap between K=1 and K=5 is large and consistent: every Tier B method gains roughly 14–19 Fine-\nGrained points by K=5 (e.g. SSLAM: 40.1% → 54.2%; Whisper-AT: 31.7% → 50.8%), indicating the correct\nanswer is frequently present in these models’ ranked output even when their single top choice misses. Most\nof that gain arrives by K=3; going from K=3 to K=5 adds a further 4–6 points, a diminishing but still\nreal return. At K=5, SSLAM’s Fine-Grained accuracy (54.2%) approaches CLAP’s Top-1 figure (54.5%,\nTable 12), is on par with the strongest non-preview Tier A models (gemini-2.5-pro, 54.1%) and trails\nonly gemini-3.1-pro-preview (61.5%) - though this is not an apples-to-apples comparision: K=5 credits a\nmethod for having the right answer anywhere in five guesses, a substantially easier bar than the single forced\nanswer every other tier is held to.\n  For the detailed per-class view, we focus the K=5 (best-case) confusion matrices in Appendix D, alongside\nthe standard Top-1 matrices, since K=5 is the most informative depth for characterizing what each Tier B\nmethod’s ranked output is actually capable of resolving.\n\n5.5. Reasoning-Strategy Analysis (Gemini Models)\n\n5.5.1. Verbosity does not predict accuracy.Table 5 and Figure 2 show average chain-of-thought response\nlength by model. The most accurate model, gemini-3.1-pro-preview, is also the most concise — nearly\n7× shorter than gemini-2.5-pro, which writes exhaustive, structured, multi-step analyses (87.6% of its\nresponses use explicit numbered steps, vs. 0% for gemini-3.1-pro-preview) without converting that extra\nlength into better fine-grained F1 than the far terser gemini-3.5-flash (Table 3). Response length and\naccuracy are, if anything, inversely related across these four models.\n\nTable 5: Average chain-of-thought response length by model.\n\n                          Model                               Avg. words / response\n                          gemini-3.1-pro-preview                          52\n                          gemini-3.5-flash                                67\n                          gemini-2.5-flash                               269\n                          gemini-2.5-pro                                 360\n\n\n\n\nSound Source Identification Benchmark                                                                            10\n\f                                                                                              August 4, 2026\n\n\n\n\nFigure 2: Average chain-of-thought response length by model (same data as Table 5).\n\n\n5.5.2. An apparent strategy-accuracy pattern is a diﬀiculty confound.Each response was tagged\nwith a primary reasoning strategy. Figure 3 shows the distribution: detailed acoustic analysis (explicit\ndiscussion of frequency, timbre, envelope, pitch) dominates for every model, from 78.1% (gemini-2.5-flash)\nup to 95.2% (gemini-3.5-flash) of responses. Holistic pattern match (a quick ”this sounds like X” judgment\nwith no feature breakdown) is the clear second-most-common strategy for all four models, but its share varies\nconsiderably: gemini-3.1-pro-preview uses it in 16.5% of responses, three to eight times more often than\nthe other three models (1.9–5.5%). The remaining strategies (process of elimination, direct assertion, non-\ngrounded) are minor and inconsistent across models, appearing in at most 13.9% of any one model’s responses.\n  Naively cross-referencing strategy against fine-grained accuracy (Table 6, visualized in Figure 4) shows\nthe same pattern in all four models: responses tagged holistic pattern match are correct 67.7–79.1% of the\ntime, versus only 39.7–59.1% for detailed acoustic analysis — a 14.3–28.0 point gap in every model, with no\nexceptions.\n\n\n\n\nSound Source Identification Benchmark                                                                     11\n\f                                                                                                         August 4, 2026\n\n\n\n\nFigure 3: Primary reasoning strategy distribution by model. Detailed acoustic analysis dominates throughout (78.1–\n95.2%); holistic pattern match is a distant second everywhere, but far more common for gemini-3.1-pro-preview\n(16.5%) than the other three models (1.9–5.5%).\n\n\nTable 6: Fine-grained accuracy by primary reasoning strategy, for the two dominant strategies, per model. n is the\nnumber of responses tagged with that strategy (out of 2,242 per model); the holistic-pattern-match bar is taller than the\ndetailed-acoustic-analysis bar in every model.\n\n Model                            Detailed-analysis acc.      Holistic-match acc. (n)             Gap (points)\n                                           (n)\n\n gemini-3.1-pro-preview                 59.1% (n=1,871)             73.4% (n=369)                      14.3\n gemini-2.5-pro                         53.5% (n=2,122)             79.1% (n=43)                       25.6\n gemini-3.5-flash                       52.4% (n=2,135)             78.8% (n=85)                       26.4\n gemini-2.5-flash                       39.7% (n=1,750)             67.7% (n=124)                      28.0\n\n\n\n\nSound Source Identification Benchmark                                                                                 12\n\f                                                                                                    August 4, 2026\n\n\n\n\nFigure 4: Fine-grained accuracy conditioned on primary reasoning strategy, per model (exact values in Table 6). In\nevery model the holistic-pattern-match bar is taller than the detailed-acoustic-analysis bar.\n\n\n   Read at face value, this looks like ”less analysis produces better answers.” We do not believe that is the\ncorrect interpretation. Restricting the comparison to gemini-3.1-pro-preview — the model with by far the\nhighest holistic-match rate (Figure 3) — and conditioning on individual classes (Table 7) shows that the two\nstrategies score about the same within a given class (e.g., 100.0% vs. 98.9% on Yell); the apparent strategy\neffect disappears once class diﬀiculty is held fixed.\n\nTable 7: Fine-grained accuracy by reasoning strategy, within class, for gemini-3.1-pro-preview. These three classes\naccount for 47% of all holistic pattern match responses from this model.\n\n Class                                  Overall accuracy    Detailed-analysis acc.     Holistic-match acc. (n)\n                                                                     (n)\n Yell - Yell                                 99.0%               100.0% (n=9)                 98.9% (n=89)\n Appliance Alarm                             93.0%               90.7% (n=54)                 95.7% (n=46)\n Water Running - Close                       80.0%               78.7% (n=61)                 82.1% (n=39)\n Source\n\n\n  The more plausible explanation, consistent with Table 7: models default to a quick holistic judgment\nprecisely when a sound is unambiguous, and reach for detailed acoustic analysis when it is genuinely hard\nto place. The reasoning strategy is a symptom of per-sample diﬀiculty, not a cause of the outcome — but it\nremains a useful signal: a model defaulting to detailed analysis is implicitly flagging ”this one was hard.”\n\n5.5.3. Confidence calibration is essentially absent.We measured how often a model’s language reads\nas confident even when its final answer is wrong (Table 8, Figure 5). Confidence level is inferred purely from\nphrasing (e.g., ”clearly,” ”definitely” vs. ”possibly,” ”might be”) by the second-pass classifier — models were\nnever asked to self-report a confidence score.\n\n\n\n\nSound Source Identification Benchmark                                                                           13\n\f                                                                                                     August 4, 2026\n\n\nTable 8: Share of wrong fine-grained answers still stated with high-confidence language.\n\n                    Model                                       Wrong answers stated with\n                                                                 high-confidence language\n                    gemini-3.5-flash                                        100.0%\n                    gemini-3.1-pro-preview                                   99.9%\n                    gemini-2.5-pro                                           97.8%\n                    gemini-2.5-flash                                         92.1%\n\n\n\n\nFigure 5: Share of wrong answers still stated with high-confidence language, by model (same data as Table 8).\n\n\n   As Table 8 and Figure 5 show, all four models are essentially uniform on this measure — the range spans\nonly 92.1% to 100.0%, with no model showing meaningfully better calibration than the others. Hedging\nlanguage (”possibly,” ”might,” ”uncertain”) appears less than once per response on average for every model.\nThere is effectively no verbal signal in these chain-of-thought traces that reliably distinguishes a correct\nanswer from an incorrect one; a system that needs to know when to defer to a human cannot obtain that\nsignal from a model’s stated confidence on this task.\n\n\n6. Discussion\n6.1. What Can and Cannot Be Compared Directly\n  The four-tier structure in Section 4 exists because a single flat ranking across all eleven methods would\noverstate precision. The defensible comparisons are within a tier: which Tier A method is best (four Gemini\nmodels plus Kimi-Audio, all given the identical closed-set prompt), and which fixed-vocabulary tagger is best\n(Tier B). Tier C (CLAP) and Tier D (BAT) are each a category of one in this study and are reported for\ndirectional context rather than as ranked competitors.\n   That said, one cross-tier observation is robust enough to be worth surfacing directionally: at the category\nlevel, task-agnostic methods that never saw the candidate list (SSLAM, PANNs in Tier B; CLAP in Tier C)\nmatch or exceed the best task-aware Tier A model’s F1, and PANNs approches it. At the fine-grained level,\nthis reverses — the best Tier A model and CLAP (Tier C) pull ahead of every Tier B tagger by 12+ F1\n\n\nSound Source Identification Benchmark                                                                           14\n\f                                                                                                August 4, 2026\n\n\n\npoints. A plausible reading is that coarse categorization is largely an acoustic pattern-matching problem, at\nwhich purpose-built audio encoders (with or without a candidate list) are already strong, while fine-grained\nsub-type disambiguation increasingly draws on the kind of contextual, elimination-based reasoning that Tier\nA’s language-generation step provides and Tier B’s fixed classification head does not. We stress this is a\nhypothesis suggested by the data, not a controlled causal claim, since Tier A and Tier B differ in task\nframing as well as in architecture.\n  Even within Tier A, the five methods are not architecturally uniform: the four Gemini models are large\nproprietary systems of undisclosed scale, while Kimi-Audio-7B-Instruct is a 7B-parameter open-weight model.\nThat it lands mid-pack in Tier A rather than at the bottom is itself informative — task-aware closed-set\nprompting appears to narrow, though not eliminate, the gap between an open 7B audio-native model and\nmuch larger proprietary multimodal systems on this task — but its lower reasoning-instruction compliance\n(Section 4) means its Tier A ranking reflects both classification ability and instruction- following, in a way\nthe four Gemini models’ more consistent compliance does not.\n\n6.2. Practical Guidance\n   For a use case that only needs the broad category (is this a siren or a doorbell?), several method families\nare viable, and a task-agnostic tagger (SSLAM, PANNs) or a zero-shot model (CLAP) may be suﬀicient\nwithout needing an LLM call per sample. For a use case needing the exact sub-type, the strongest current\nmethods top out around 51–57% F1, and Tier A (task-aware LLM with an explicit candidate list) or Tier C\n(CLAP) are the strongest available options; expect specific, predictable failure modes — recording-distance\nand directionality judgments, and acoustically adjacent sub-types within a category — rather than random\nerror. If an open-weight, self-hostable option is a requirement, Kimi-Audio-7B-Instruct is a usable Tier A\nalternative to the Gemini models, at a cost in fine-grained F1 (32.9% vs. 56.7% for the best Gemini model)\nand in reliability (1.6% of samples yield no scoreable answer at all, versus 0% for the Gemini models), both\nworth budgeting for in a production deployment.\n\n6.3. Limitations\n• Tier C’s task framing partially overlaps Tier A’s. CLAP is given the same 23 candidates as the\n  Gemini models (as text), which is part of why its numbers are more directly comparable to Tier A than\n  Tier B’s are; this is noted explicitly rather than treated as a hidden asymmetry, but it means CLAP’s\n  strong fine-grained score should not be read as evidence that zero-shot similarity generally rivals LLM\n  reasoning on unconstrained audio tagging.\n• Tier D’s scoring depends on a judge model. BAT’s free-text answers are graded by gemini-2.5-\n  flash-lite rather than exact match; judge leniency or strictness is a source of variance this study does\n  not independently calibrate against a second judge or human raters.\n• Tier B’s mapping is post-hoc. The AudioSet-to-taxonomy mapping used for YAMNet, PANNs,\n  Whisper-AT, and SSLAM is hand-constructed and tagged exact/approximate at data-preparation time;\n  a different, equally reasonable mapping could shift these numbers somewhat, though the relative ordering\n  within Tier B was stable under manual review.\n• BAT’s spatial-audio design is not exercised here. BAT’s Spatial-AST encoder expects binaural\n  input with directional cues; our clips are effectively mono, so the channel was duplicated rather than\n  fabricating synthetic spatial cues. This is an honest ”no directional information” representation but\n  means BAT is evaluated outside its primary designed use case.\n• Kimi-Audio’s 36 excluded samples are a genuine reliability difference, not a scoring artifact.\n  We confirmed this by re-attempting extraction with three successive rounds of parser improvements and\n  by re-running the 36 samples under identical settings, which reproduced the same failures rather than\n  resolving them (Kimi-Audio’s text decoding is greedy, so a same-settings retry has limited ability to\n  escape a repetition-loop or refusal outcome). We did not pursue a targeted fix (e.g., raising the text\n  repetition penalty, currently uninformatively set to 1.0) specifically for these samples, since doing so\n  would apply different generation settings to a subset of Kimi-Audio’s data than to the rest, undermining\n  the like-for-like comparison this study is built around.\n\n\nSound Source Identification Benchmark                                                                      15\n\f                                                                                                  August 4, 2026\n\n\n\n• Single dataset, single run. All numbers reflect one balanced 23-class dataset and a single evaluation\n  pass per method (no repeated sampling or multiple-seed variance estimates), and the 11-category taxon-\n  omy reflects the specific sound sources of interest to this study rather than a general-purpose acoustic\n  scene ontology.\n\n6.4. Future Work\n  Natural extensions include: a second, independent LLM judge (or a small human-rated subset) to quantify\nTier D’s judge-variance; testing whether few-shot prompting can induce BAT toward closed-set letter selection\nrather than its default binary yes/no bias; and extending the tiered framework to acoustic scenes with genuine\nspatial/directional structure, which would let BAT’s native architecture be evaluated on-task rather than\ndegraded to mono input.\n   A further direction motivated by concurrent work (Section 2) is to manipulate the audio front end itself as\nan independent variable within Tier A. All Tier A methods in this study are encoder-based to some degree\n— Kimi-Audio explicitly tokenizes audio through a pretrained Whisper-derived encoder before its LLM core\never sees it, and Gemini’s proprietary audio pathway is almost certainly similar in spirit. Fan et al. [4] report\nthat removing this encoder entirely (projecting Mel-spectrogram patches directly into the LLM) improves\nperformance specifically on tasks carried by low-level acoustic structure, including environmental sound\nclassification, at some cost to knowledge-intensive tasks. Our own fine-grained results are dominated by\nexactly this kind of acoustic discrimination — distinguishing Siren - Police from Siren - Ambulance, or\na wireless doorbell chime from a mechanical one, plausibly turns on preserved pitch contour and timbre rather\nthan semantic content. Whether an encoder-free architecture narrows Kimi-Audio’s fine-grained gap to the\nGemini models specifically on these acoustically-driven distinctions, or whether Gemini’s advantage instead\nreflects sheer model scale or training data, is a testable question our tiered framework is directly set up to\nanswer, given access to a Mel-LLM-style checkpoint evaluated under the same closed-set prompt.\n\n\n7. Conclusion\n   Comparing eleven audio classification methods spanning four fundamentally different evaluation paradigms\non the identical 2,242-sample, 23-class dataset shows that no single method dominates once category and fine-\ngrained granularity, and task framing, are both accounted for. Task-aware closed-set LLM selection (gemini-\n3.1-pro-preview) gives the best fine-grained F1 (56.7%) of any method tested, but task-agnostic fixed-\nvocabulary taggers and zero-shot audio-text similarity are competitive or superior at the coarser category\nlevel despite never seeing the candidate list. The open-weight Kimi-Audio-7B-Instruct, evaluated under the\nidentical closed-set prompt, reaches a competitive 67.5%/32.9% category/fine- grained F1 for its size, though\nwith a measurably higher rate of unscoreable responses (1.6% of samples) than the four Gemini models (0%),\nreflecting less consistent instruction-following rather than a scoring artifact. The category-to-fine-grained\naccuracy gap is concentrated in a small number of structurally hard, consistent failure modes rather than\ndiffuse noise, and these generalize to Kimi-Audio, in some cases (Siren sub-types) more extremely than any\nGemini model. Separately, analyzing the Gemini models’ own chain-of-thought reveals that more reasoning\ntext does not buy more accuracy, that an apparent reasoning-strategy effect on accuracy is confounded by\nper-sample diﬀiculty, and — most consequential for downstream use — that these models give almost no\nusable signal, in their stated confidence, of when they are wrong.\n\n\nEthics Statement\n  This study evaluates model performance on a curated set of short environmental sound recordings (e.g.,\ndoorbells, sirens, appliance sounds) and involves no human subjects, personal data, or sensitive content.\n\n\n\n\nSound Source Identification Benchmark                                                                         16\n\f                                                                                              August 4, 2026\n\n\n\nReferences\n [1] Sajjad Abdoli, Patrick Cardinal, and Alessandro Lameiras Koerich. End-to-end environmental sound\n     classification using a 1d convolutional neural network. Expert Systems with Applications, 136:252–263,\n     2019.\n [2] Tony Alex, Sara Atito, Armin Mustafa, Muhammad Awais, and Philip J. B. Jackson. Sslam: Enhancing\n     self-supervised models with audio mixtures for polyphonic soundscapes. In International Conference on\n     Learning Representations (ICLR), 2025.\n [3] Benjamin Elizalde, Soham Deshmukh, and Huaming Wang. Natural language supervision for general-\n     purpose audio representations. In Proceedings of the IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech\n     and Signal Processing (ICASSP), pages 336–340, 2024.\n [4] Ruchao Fan, Yiming Wang, Yuxuan Hu, Bo Ren, Yufei Xia, Xiaofei Wang, Yao Qian, Shujie Liu, and\n     Jinyu Li. Llm can read spectrogram: Encoder-free speech-language modeling, 2026.\n [5] Google Gemini Team, Gheorghe Comanici, Eric Bieber, Mike Schaekermann, et al. Gemini 2.5: Push-\n     ing the frontier with advanced reasoning, multimodality, long context, and next generation agentic\n     capabilities, 2025.\n\n [6] Jort F. Gemmeke, Daniel P. W. Ellis, Dylan Freedman, Aren Jansen, Wade Lawrence, R. Channing\n     Moore, Manoj Plakal, and Marvin Ritter. Audio set: An ontology and human-labeled dataset for audio\n     events. In Proceedings of the IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing\n     (ICASSP), pages 776–780, 2017.\n [7] Yuan Gong, Sameer Khurana, Leonid Karlinsky, and James Glass. Whisper-at: Noise-robust automatic\n     speech recognizers are also strong general audio event taggers. In Proc. Interspeech 2023, pages 2798–\n     2802, 2023.\n [8] Yuan Gong, Hongyin Luo, Alexander H. Liu, Leonid Karlinsky, and James Glass. Listen, think, and\n     understand. In International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR), 2024.\n [9] Google Research. Yamnet. https://github.com/tensorflow/models/tree/master/research/au\n     dioset/yamnet, 2020. Pretrained audio event classification model released in the TensorFlow Models\n     repository, trained on AudioSet using the architecture of Hershey et al. [10].\n[10] Shawn Hershey, Sourish Chaudhuri, Daniel P. W. Ellis, Jort F. Gemmeke, Aren Jansen, R. Channing\n     Moore, Manoj Plakal, Devin Platt, Rif A. Saurous, Bryan Seybold, Malcolm Slaney, Ron J. Weiss, and\n     Kevin W. Wilson. Cnn architectures for large-scale audio classification. In Proceedings of the IEEE\n     International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP), pages 131–135, 2017.\n\n[11] KimiTeam, Ding Ding, Zeqian Ju, Yichong Leng, Songxiang Liu, Tong Liu, Zeyu Shang, Kai Shen, Wei\n     Song, Xu Tan, Heyi Tang, Zhengtao Wang, Chu Wei, Yifei Xin, Xinran Xu, Jianwei Yu, Yutao Zhang,\n     Xinyu Zhou, Y. Charles, et al. Kimi-audio technical report, 2025.\n[12] Qiuqiang Kong, Yin Cao, Turab Iqbal, Yuxuan Wang, Wenwu Wang, and Mark D. Plumbley. Panns:\n     Large-scale pretrained audio neural networks for audio pattern recognition. IEEE/ACM Transactions\n     on Audio, Speech, and Language Processing, 28:2880–2894, 2020.\n[13] Karol J. Piczak. Esc: Dataset for environmental sound classification. In Proceedings of the 23rd ACM\n     International Conference on Multimedia, pages 1015–1018, 2015.\n[14] Zhisheng Zheng, Puyuan Peng, Ziyang Ma, Xie Chen, Eunsol Choi, and David Harwath. Bat: Learning\n     to reason about spatial sounds with large language models. In Proceedings of the 41st International\n     Conference on Machine Learning (ICML), volume 235 of Proceedings of Machine Learning Research,\n     pages 61454–61469, 2024.\n\n\n\nSound Source Identification Benchmark                                                                    17\n\f                                                                                              August 4, 2026\n\n\n\nA. The 23 Fine-Grained Candidate Classes\n  Table 9 lists the exact 23 lettered candidates given to every Tier A (Gemini) model for every sample, and\nto Tier C (CLAP) as natural-language phrasings of the same 23 classes. These collapse into the 11 broader\nCategory-Level classes: Appliance Alarm, Cat Sounds, Clap, Dog Sounds, Doorbell, Hairdryer, Kettle Boiling,\nKnock, Siren, Water Running, Yell.\n\nTable 9: The 23 fine-grained candidate classes.\n", "numLines": 450, "startLine": 400, "totalLines": 1330}}}
