{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-936ac580c95afbed", "type": "tool_result", "content": "850\t                             Letter           sound_type           sub_category\n851\t                                A             Appliance Alarm      Appliance Alarm\n852\t                                B             Cat Sounds           Meow\n853\t                                C             Cat Sounds           Purr\n854\t                                D             Clap                 Double Clap\n855\t                                E             Clap                 Single Clap\n856\t                                F             Clap                 Triple Clap\n857\t                                G             Dog Sounds           Growl\n858\t                                H             Dog Sounds           Howl\n859\t                                I             Doorbell             Digital\n860\t                                J             Doorbell             Mechanical\n861\t                                K             Doorbell             Wireless\n862\t                                L             Hairdryer            Hair Dryer\n863\t                                M             Kettle Boiling       Boiling\n864\t                                N             Kettle Boiling       Whistling\n865\t                                O             Knock                Door Knock\n866\t                                P             Knock                Surface Knock\n867\t                                Q             Siren                Ambulance\n868\t                                R             Siren                Fire\n869\t                                S             Siren                Other\n870\t                                T             Siren                Police\n871\t                                U             Water Running        Close Source\n872\t                                V             Water Running        Distant Source\n873\t                                W             Yell                 Yell\n874\t\n875\t\n876\t\n877\tB. Exact Prompts Used\n878\t  This appendix gives the verbatim text prompts used for every method that consumes a text input (Tier\n879\tA, Tier C, and Tier D; Tier B methods receive no text prompt at all and are omitted). All prompts are\n880\treproduced exactly as sent, with no paraphrasing.\n881\t\n882\tB.1. Tier A: Candidate-Selection Prompt (Gemini and Kimi-Audio)\n883\t  The identical prompt below was used for all four Gemini models and for Kimi-Audio-7B-Instruct (Section 4).\n884\t{candidate_list} is substituted with the 23-line lettered list given in Appendix A.\n885\t\n886\t      Listen carefully to the audio file provided.\n887\t      Tell me what you hear. You are free to use any signal processing capability or internal\n888\t      sound classification system you may have access to. Tell me what approach you used, and\n889\t      share your full chain of thought.\n890\t      Choose the single candidate below that best identifies the true source of the sound. You\n891\t      MUST pick exactly one letter from this list (use the exact letter):\n892\t      {candidate_list}\n893\t      After your analysis, provide your final answer on the very last line in this exact JSON\n894\t      format (no markdown, no extra text after it):\n895\t\n896\t\n897\tSound Source Identification Benchmark                                                                    18\n898\t\f                                                                                            August 4, 2026\n899\t\n900\t\n901\t\n902\t      {\"answer\": \"X\"}\n903\t      Where X is the single capital letter of your chosen candidate.\n904\t\n905\t\n906\tB.2. Tier C: CLAP Candidate Phrasings\n907\t  CLAP does not receive a generative prompt; instead, each of the 23 candidates (same letter assignment as\n908\tAppendix A) is given to CLAP as a short natural-language phrase, embedded once, and compared against\n909\tthe audio embedding via cosine similarity.\n910\t\n911\tTable 10: The 23 candidate phrasings given to CLAP.\n912\t\n913\t            Letter               sound_type           sub_category              CLAP phrase\n914\t              A                  Appliance Alarm      Appliance Alarm           the sound of an appliance\n915\t                                                                                alarm beeping\n916\t              B                  Cat Sounds           Meow                      a cat meowing\n917\t              C                  Cat Sounds           Purr                      a cat purring\n918\t              D                  Clap                 Double Clap               two hands clapping twice\n919\t                                                                                in quick succession\n920\t              E                  Clap                 Single Clap               a single hand clap\n921\t              F                  Clap                 Triple Clap               hands clapping three times\n922\t                                                                                in quick succession\n923\t              G                  Dog Sounds           Growl                     a dog growling\n924\t              H                  Dog Sounds           Howl                      a dog howling\n925\t              I                  Doorbell             Digital                   a digital doorbell chime\n926\t              J                  Doorbell             Mechanical                a mechanical doorbell\n927\t                                                                                ringing\n928\t              K                  Doorbell             Wireless                  a wireless doorbell chime\n929\t              L                  Hairdryer            Hair Dryer                a hair dryer blowing\n930\t              M                  Kettle Boiling       Boiling                   water boiling in a kettle\n931\t              N                  Kettle Boiling       Whistling                 a kettle whistling\n932\t              O                  Knock                Door Knock                someone knocking on a\n933\t                                                                                door\n934\t              P                  Knock                Surface Knock             someone knocking on a\n935\t                                                                                surface\n936\t              Q                  Siren                Ambulance                 an ambulance siren\n937\t              R                  Siren                Fire                      a fire truck siren\n938\t              S                  Siren                Other                     a generic emergency siren\n939\t              T                  Siren                Police                    a police car siren\n940\t              U                  Water Running        Close Source              water running close to the\n941\t                                                                                microphone\n942\t              V                  Water Running        Distant Source            water running far from the\n943\t                                                                                microphone\n944\t              W                  Yell                 Yell                      a person yelling\n945\t\n946\t\n947\tB.3. Tier D: BAT’s Open-Vocabulary Question\n948\t  Unlike Tier A, BAT receives no candidate list and no format constraint. The entire prompt is a fixed,\n949\tgeneric question, identical for every sample:\n950\t\n951\t      Enumerate the sound occurrences in the audio clip.\n952\t\n953\t\n954\tB.4. Tier D: LLM Judge Prompt\n955\t  BAT’s free-text answers are not scored by exact match; they are graded by an independent LLM judge\n956\t(gemini-2.5-flash-lite), given the true label and BAT’s raw answer, with the following prompt:\n957\t\n958\t\n959\tSound Source Identification Benchmark                                                                    19\n960\t\f                                                                                            August 4, 2026\n961\t\n962\t\n963\t\n964\t      You are evaluating whether an audio model's free-text description of a sound correctly\n965\t      identifies its true source.\n966\t      True label:\n967\t        sound_type = \"{true_sound_type}\"\n968\t        sub_category = \"{true_sub_category}\"\n969\t      The model's free-text answer:\n970\t      ---\n971\t      {answer}\n972\t      ---\n973\t      Does this answer correctly identify the sound as being a \"{true_sound_type}\", specifi-\n974\t      cally \"{true_sub_category}\"? Consider synonyms and paraphrases. Judge category-level\n975\t      (broad sound_type match) and fine-grained (specific sub_category match) separately.\n976\t      Respond with ONLY this JSON on the last line (no markdown):\n977\t      {\"category_correct\": true_or_false, \"finegrained_correct\": true_or_false, \"reasoning\":\n978\t      \"one sentence\"}\n979\t\n980\t\n981\tC. Complete AudioSet-to-Taxonomy Mapping (Tier B)\n982\t  Table 11 lists every entry in the lookup table used to translate YAMNet/PANNs/Whisper-AT/SSLAM’s\n983\tnative AudioSet labels into our 23-class taxonomy, as described in Section 4.2.1. Sub-category “—” denotes\n984\ta category-only entry (the class collapses to Category-Level only; see Section 4).\n985\t\n986\t\n987\t\n988\t\n989\tSound Source Identification Benchmark                                                                  20\n990\t\f                                                                                                     August 4, 2026\n991\t\n992\t\n993\tTable 11: Complete AudioSet-to-taxonomy mapping, all 22 entries.\n994\t\n995\t    AudioSet label                      sound_type       sub_category      Quality       Reason (if approxi-\n996\t                                                                                         mate)\n997\t    \"Alarm\"                             Appliance        Appliance Alarm   approximate   Generic parent\n998\t                                        Alarm                                            class; also covers\n999\t                                                                                         car/smoke/clock\n1000\t                                                                                         alarms\n1001\t    \"Meow\"                              Cat Sounds       Meow              exact         —\n1002\t    \"Purr\"                              Cat Sounds       Purr              exact         —\n1003\t    \"Clapping\"                          Clap             —                 exact         AudioSet has no clap-\n1004\t                                                                                         count distinction (sin-\n1005\t                                                                                         gle/double/triple)\n1006\t    \"Growling\"                          Dog Sounds       Growl             exact         —\n1007\t    \"Howl\"                              Dog Sounds       Howl              exact         —\n1008\t    \"Bark\"                              Dog Sounds       —                 approximate   Generic bark, not spe-\n1009\t                                                                                         cific to growl/howl\n1010\t    \"Doorbell\"                          Doorbell         —                 exact         AudioSet has no dig-\n1011\t                                                                                         ital, mechanical, or\n1012\t                                                                                         wireless distinction\n1013\t    \"Hair dryer\"                        Hairdryer        Hair Dryer        exact         —\n1014\t    \"Boiling\"                           Kettle Boiling   Boiling           exact         —\n1015\t    \"Steam whistle\"                     Kettle Boiling   Whistling         exact         —\n1016\t    \"Whistling\"                         Kettle Boiling   Whistling         approximate   Generic whistling, not\n1017\t                                                                                         specific to a boiling\n1018\t                                                                                         kettle\n1019\t    \"Knock\"                             Knock            —                 exact         AudioSet has no\n1020\t                                                                                         door/surface distinc-\n1021\t                                                                                         tion\n1022\t    \"Police car (siren)\"                Siren            Police            exact         —\n1023\t    \"Ambulance (siren)\"                 Siren            Ambulance         exact         —\n1024\t    \"Fire engine, fire truck            Siren            Fire              exact         —\n1025\t    (siren)\"\n1026\t    \"Siren\"                             Siren            Other             approximate   Generic siren, not\n1027\t                                                                                         vehicle-type-specific\n1028\t    \"Civil defense siren\"               Siren            Other             approximate   A distinct siren type\n1029\t                                                                                         with no closer match\n1030\t                                                                                         available\n1031\t    \"Water tap, faucet\"                 Water Running    —                 exact         AudioSet has no\n1032\t                                                                                         recording-distance\n1033\t                                                                                         distinction\n1034\t    \"Yell\"                              Yell             Yell              exact         —\n1035\t    \"Shout\"                             Yell             Yell              approximate   Near-synonym, not\n1036\t                                                                                         an identical acoustic\n1037\t                                                                                         concept\n1038\t    \"Bellow\"                            Yell             Yell              approximate   Near-synonym, not\n1039\t                                                                                         an identical acoustic\n1040\t                                                                                         concept\n1041\t\n1042\t\n1043\t\n1044\tD. Complete Results: Accuracy, Per-Class Metrics, and Confusion Matrices\n1045\tD.1. Raw Accuracy (for reference)\n1046\t  Table 12 reports raw accuracy for all eleven methods, for direct comparability with the project’s earlier\n1047\taccuracy-based reporting. As discussed in Section 4, we treat macro F1/TPR/FNR (Tables 2 and 3) as the\n1048\tprimary metrics; accuracy is provided here as a secondary, widely-recognized reference point.\n1049\t\n1050\t\n1051\tSound Source Identification Benchmark                                                                              21\n1052\t\f                                                                                                 August 4, 2026\n1053\t\n1054\t\n1055\tTable 12: Raw accuracy, all eleven methods, both granularities. N =2,242 for every method except Kimi-Audio\n1056\t(N =2,206, marked ‡).\n1057\t\n1058\t Tier                            Method                       Category Acc.            Fine-Grained Acc.\n1059\t A                               gemini-3.1-pro-preview            86.8%                      61.5%\n1060\t A                               gemini-2.5-pro                    78.5%                      54.1%\n1061\t A                               gemini-3.5-flash                  78.4%                      53.4%\n1062\t A                               kimi-audio-7b-instruct‡           71.8%                      40.8%\n1063\t A                               gemini-2.5-flash                  68.6%                      42.0%\n1064\t B                               sslam                             88.4%                      40.1%\n1065\t B                               panns                             84.3%                      37.3%\n1066\t B                               whisper-at                        77.8%                      31.7%\n1067\t B                               yamnet                            52.1%                      26.5%\n1068\t C                               clap                              89.1%                      54.5%\n1069\t D                               bat (judge, micro)                62.4%                      35.9%\n1070\t\n1071\t\n1072\tD.2. Confusion Matrices\n1073\t   Figures 7–28 give the row-normalized (recall) confusion matrix for every method with a discrete predicted\n1074\tclass (all methods except BAT, Tier D, for which no confusion matrix can be constructed — see Section 4),\n1075\tat both the category and fine-grained level.\n1076\t\n1077\t\n1078\t\n1079\t\n1080\tFigure 6: Fine-grained (23-class) confusion matrix (row-normalized recall) for gemini-3.1-pro-preview, the best-\n1081\tperforming Tier A model. This is the detailed view behind the class-level failures discussed in Section 5.3.\n1082\t\n1083\t\n1084\tSound Source Identification Benchmark                                                                        22\n1085\t\f                                                                         August 4, 2026\n1086\t\n1087\t\n1088\t\n1089\t\n1090\tFigure 7: gemini